consiSt("id",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Idaho Supreme Court","d":"Court of last resort, with five justices who choose a chief justice from among themselves. Every appeal is filed as an appeal to the Supreme Court; the Court keeps some and assigns others to the Court of Appeals under I.A.R. 108. Hears appeals from district courts, the Public Utilities Commission, and the Industrial Commission (workers’ compensation), reviews Court of Appeals decisions on petition for review, issues special writs, answers certified questions from federal courts, adopts court rules, and regulates the practice of law."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Idaho Court of Appeals","d":"Four judges sitting in panels of three. Hears only the cases the Supreme Court assigns to it. All filings after the notice of appeal go to the Clerk of the Supreme Court, who is also the Clerk of the Court of Appeals, under the Supreme Court caption and number."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"District Courts (seven judicial districts)","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts. Hear felonies, civil actions over $10,000, appeals from the magistrate division, and judicial review of agency decisions. One set of civil rules applies statewide in district and magistrate courts."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Magistrate Division of the District Courts","d":"Part of the district court, not a separate court. Magistrate judges hear civil actions up to $10,000, small claims, evictions, probate, guardianships, family law (under the separate Idaho Rules of Family Law Procedure), juvenile cases, misdemeanors, infractions, and felony preliminary hearings. Most magistrate decisions are appealed to the district judge first (I.R.C.P. 83), not to the Supreme Court."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (effective January 1, 2026)","d":"Idaho adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (UPEPA) in 2025, effective January 1, 2026. It applies to civil actions filed, and causes of action asserted, on or after January 1, 2026; claims asserted earlier are not affected. Before this Act, the Idaho Code chapter list shows no general anti-SLAPP statute.","c":"Idaho Code §§ 6-3901, 6-3913, 6-3914 (2025 Idaho Sess. Laws ch. 17, § 1)","v":true},{"t":"Scope: three kinds of protected activity","d":"Covers any cause of action (including a cross-claim, counterclaim, or third-party claim) against a person based on: (a) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (b) communication on an issue under consideration or review in such a proceeding; or (c) exercise of freedom of speech or press, assembly, petition, or association under the U.S. or Idaho Constitution on a matter of public concern.\nExclusions: claims against a governmental unit or its employees or agents acting officially; government enforcement actions against an imminent threat to public health or safety; and claims against a seller or lessor of goods or services arising from a communication about its own goods or services. Creating or promoting dramatic, literary, musical, political, journalistic, or artistic works is not “goods or services.” The Act must be broadly construed to protect these rights. Idaho added § 6-3902(4): a cause of action under the chapter is subject to Idaho Supreme Court rules.","c":"Idaho Code §§ 6-3902, 6-3911","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: 60 days to file, automatic stay, 60-day hearing and ruling","d":"File a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after being served with the pleading that asserts the claim, or later for good cause. Filing automatically stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery and pending hearings or motions, until the court rules and the time to appeal runs; on motion, the court may also stay related matters involving other parties. Limited discovery is allowed only if specific information is needed to meet a burden and is not otherwise reasonably available.\nThe court must hear the motion within 60 days after filing (later for allowed discovery or other good cause) and rule within 60 days after the hearing. Not stayed: fee motions, voluntary dismissals, severance, and, for good cause, unrelated motions and public-safety injunctions.","c":"Idaho Code §§ 6-3903, 6-3904, 6-3905, 6-3908","v":true},{"t":"Standard: dismissal with prejudice","d":"The court considers the pleadings, the motion papers, and any evidence that could be considered on summary judgment. It must dismiss with prejudice if the movant shows the Act applies, the responding party fails to show an exclusion, and either the responding party fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A voluntary dismissal without prejudice does not defeat the motion or fees; a voluntary dismissal with prejudice makes the movant the prevailing party.","c":"Idaho Code §§ 6-3906, 6-3907","v":true},{"t":"Fees: mandatory for a prevailing movant","d":"The court must award court costs, reasonable attorney’s fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant, and to a prevailing responding party only if the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay.","c":"Idaho Code § 6-3910","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: as of right within 42 days, with a full stay","d":"A moving party may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part, within 42 days after entry of the order “pursuant to applicable court rule.” While the appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties are stayed. The Idaho Appellate Rules collected on November 10, 2026 do not mention the Act; the appeal would rest on I.A.R. 11(a)(8) (orders made appealable by statute), and the notice must cite the statute in its jurisdictional statement.","c":"Idaho Code §§ 6-3904(3), 6-3909; I.A.R. 11(a)(8), 17(g)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: motion in each case, $625 to the Bar, Idaho local counsel","d":"A lawyer who is not an Idaho Bar member and not an Idaho resident may appear in an Idaho case by court order if active and in good standing in another U.S. jurisdiction and currently maintaining an ongoing law practice in another jurisdiction. File a motion with the court that designates Idaho local counsel (with address and phone), includes local counsel’s written consent, and identifies the home bar and whether it limits pro hac vice admissions. Submit to the Idaho State Bar a $625 fee ($125 of it goes to the Idaho Law Foundation’s pro bono program), a certificate of good standing from the jurisdiction where the lawyer practices, and a copy of the motion. Serve all counsel of record and give the court a proposed order. The rule contains a form motion and form order; any prior denial of admission anywhere requires a separate explanatory affidavit.\nNote: pro hac vice is Idaho Bar Commission Rule 227 in the current rules. Rule 222 is now “Immunity” for admissions proceedings.","c":"I.B.C.R. 227(a), (c), (j), (k); I.B.C.R. 222","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel must attend everything","d":"Local counsel is an active Idaho Bar member with whom the court and opposing counsel can readily communicate, and must personally appear with the pro hac vice lawyer on all matters before the court unless specifically excused by the trial judge. Every filing that names the pro hac vice lawyer must give that lawyer’s current office address in the home jurisdiction.","c":"I.B.C.R. 227(b), (f)","v":true},{"t":"No numerical cap, except reciprocity","d":"Idaho places no limit on the number of pro hac vice admissions, except that a lawyer applying on a license from a jurisdiction that caps admissions of Idaho lawyers faces the same cap in Idaho. Agencies may admit out-of-state lawyers using the same standards. The applicant consents to disciplinary jurisdiction of the court and the Bar and commits to follow the Idaho Standards for Civility in Professional Conduct.","c":"I.B.C.R. 227(d), (h), (i)","v":true},{"t":"Appeals: separate motion to argue, 14 days ahead, unless already admitted","d":"To argue before the Supreme Court, a licensed Idaho lawyer must move at least 14 days before the hearing or argument, certifying the out-of-state lawyer’s good standing, substantially in the I.B.C.R. 227(j) form. A lawyer already admitted pro hac vice in the case may continue in the Supreme Court without a new order. Every notice of appeal, petition, motion, and brief must be signed by at least one licensed Idaho attorney of record.","c":"I.A.R. 9, 11.2(a)","v":true},{"t":"Practicing without pro hac vice: Rule 5.5","d":"Idaho’s Rule 5.5(b) lets a lawyer admitted elsewhere provide services in Idaho that are authorized by pro hac vice admission under I.B.C.R. 227, are for an in-house employer, are reasonably related to a representation in the lawyer’s home jurisdiction, or are done in association with Idaho counsel who actively participates. The text has no separate ABA-style temporary-practice list, and its comments bar soliciting Idaho clients under these exceptions.","c":"Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(b) & cmts. [2]-[3]","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances: only a pro bono notice in civil court","d":"Rule 1.2(c) allows a reasonable limited-scope representation with informed consent (writing encouraged, not required). The civil rules provide a court procedure only for unpaid help: an attorney may file a notice of limited pro bono appearance for a self-represented litigant, act only on the listed matters, and end the appearance by a notice of completion without leave of court. Otherwise, withdrawal requires leave of court on a noticed motion, and the client gets 21 days to appoint new counsel or appear pro se, during which the case is stayed.","c":"Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(c) & cmt. [8]; I.R.C.P. 11.3, 11.4","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: mandatory for attorneys through iCourt File and Serve","d":"Attorneys must file electronically under the Idaho Rules for Electronic Filing and Service, which override other procedural rules on filing, format, and service. Documents must be text-searchable PDF (or PDF/A), 50 MB per file at most. A filing submitted before midnight in the court’s time zone counts for that day, and the system timestamp controls, even if the filing is placed in an error queue. Service through the system is complete when the system emails the selected service contacts; if served electronically after 5:00 p.m. local time, one day is added to the response period. A rejected or failed submission may relate back if resubmitted within 7 business days with the required notation. Appellate briefs, motions, petitions, and cost memoranda are also filed electronically.","c":"I.R.E.F.S. 1, 4(a), 6(a), 12, 14, 17(f)-(h), 20(f), 21","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Idaho Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"The Idaho Rules of Professional Conduct took effect November 1, 1986 and were revised effective July 1, 2004 after an Ethics 2000 review. They are based largely on the ABA Model Rules, with Idaho variations, and the Supreme Court also adopted the comments. The Bar’s posted version is labeled current as of March 9, 2026; the text itself shows no 2024-2026 amendment dates.","c":"Idaho R. Prof. Conduct (isb.idaho.gov version current as of March 9, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: may reveal intent to commit any crime","d":"Rule 1.6(b)(1) permits (but does not require) disclosure to prevent the client from committing a crime, including disclosure of a stated intention to commit any crime, not just crimes involving death or bodily harm. The Idaho comment says this differs from the ABA Model Rule.","c":"Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 1.6(b)(1) & cmt. [6]","v":true},{"t":"Client files: a separate Rule 1.16A","d":"Idaho has a separate client-file rule. The lawyer must make the file available to the client or former client. The file includes the lawyer’s notes whether or not they are work product. Copying may be charged only at actual cost. The file may be withheld for unpaid fees only if that will not cause imminent prejudice, and it may not be destroyed while litigation, an investigation, or discipline is pending or anticipated.","c":"Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 1.16A","v":true},{"t":"Fees: free accounting on request; no contingent fees in divorce or criminal cases","d":"On reasonable request, a lawyer must provide, without charge, an accounting of fees and costs that itemizes charges and describes the services and who performed them. The fee basis should preferably be in writing; contingent fee agreements must be in a writing signed by the client. Advance fees go into trust and are withdrawn only as earned; the text has no “nonrefundable fee” provision.","c":"Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(b)-(d), (f) & cmt. [4]; 1.15(c)","v":true},{"t":"Threatening criminal charges for civil advantage is barred","d":"Rule 4.4(a) forbids presenting, participating in presenting, or threatening criminal charges solely to gain advantage in a civil matter. It also forbids means meant to embarrass or burden others, including engendering bias based on gender, race, religion, national origin, or sexual preference. The comment calls the criminal-charge rule Idaho’s “more traditional view,” abandoned in most jurisdictions.","c":"Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 4.4(a) & cmt. [3]","v":true},{"t":"No ABA 8.4(g); settlement conditioned on dropping a bar complaint is misconduct","d":"Rule 8.4 has no harassment or discrimination paragraph like ABA Model Rule 8.4(g). Knowingly showing bias in representing a client is addressed under 8.4(d) through a comment. A comment also says conditioning a civil settlement on someone not filing, or withdrawing, a disciplinary complaint is prejudicial to the administration of justice.","c":"Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 8.4 & cmts. [3], [6]","v":true},{"t":"Advertising: older structure kept","d":"Idaho keeps separate Rules 7.4 (communicating fields of practice; a specialist claim needs certification by a Bar-approved organization) and 7.5 (firm names). It also keeps a 2-year advertising record requirement and the “Advertising Material” label on written solicitations. Rule 7.6 bars accepting government legal work or judicial appointments obtained through political contributions.","c":"Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 7.1(c), 7.2(b), 7.3(c), 7.4, 7.5, 7.6","v":true},{"t":"Sale of a practice: partial sales allowed","d":"Idaho does not require selling the entire practice or leaving practice; the seller need only stop practicing in the area of law sold. The comment says this differs from the ABA Model Rule. Client consent is presumed if the client does not object within 90 days of notice.","c":"Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 1.17(a), (c) & cmt. [2]","v":true},{"t":"Financial assistance, screening, trust records","d":"Rule 1.8(e) allows only advancing costs and paying costs for indigent clients; the text has no exception for modest gifts to pro bono clients. Lateral conflicts may be screened with written notice to the former client. Trust account records must be kept for 5 years, and trust funds must be in an account in the state where the lawyer’s office is located unless the client consents otherwise. Pro bono is aspirational at 50 hours a year.","c":"Idaho R. Prof. Conduct 1.8(e), 1.10(a)(2), 1.15(a), 6.1","v":true},{"t":"Civility standards bind pro hac vice lawyers","d":"Pro hac vice applicants commit to follow the Idaho Standards for Civility in Professional Conduct, and the form motion recites this commitment. The Standards themselves were not collected.","c":"I.B.C.R. 227(d)(2), (j)","v":true}],"style":"State of Idaho","home":"https://isc.idaho.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Disqualification without cause can expire within 7 days","d":"The peremptory motion is due 7 days after service of the first notice setting a status conference, pretrial conference, trial, or contested motion hearing, if that comes before the 21-day pleading deadline. It is lost once the judge presides over any such proceeding. Each party gets only one in the case.","c":"I.R.C.P. 40(a)(1), (4)"},{"t":"Notice of appeal: 42 days from the file stamp, filed in the trial court, jurisdictional","d":"Appeal by filing a notice with the district court clerk within 42 days from the clerk’s filing stamp on the judgment, not 30 days from entry or service. It is not filed until received, it cannot be extended, and a late notice means automatic dismissal. Rule 60 motions and cost or fee motions do not restart the clock.","c":"I.A.R. 14(a), 20, 21, 46"},{"t":"Post-judgment motions are due in 14 days, not 28","d":"Motions for new trial, to alter or amend, to amend findings, and for judgment notwithstanding the verdict are all due within 14 days after entry of judgment. A motion to reconsider an interlocutory order must come before, or within 14 days after, final judgment. The court cannot extend these times.","c":"I.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 11.2(b), 2.2(b)"},{"t":"A judgment must be a bare separate document","d":"A judgment must be titled “Judgment” or “Decree,” begin “JUDGMENT IS ENTERED AS FOLLOWS:”, and contain no recitals, reasoning, or findings. It is final only if it resolves all claims as to all parties (except costs and fees) or carries a Rule 54(b) certificate. A nonconforming order may not start the appeal clock.","c":"I.R.C.P. 54(a)-(b), 58; I.A.R. 11(a)(1), (3)"},{"t":"Costs and fees memorandum due 14 days after judgment, or waived","d":"File and serve the memorandum of costs and attorney fees, with an attorney affidavit, no later than 14 days after entry of judgment. Objections are due 14 days after service. On appeal, fees must be claimed as an issue in your first brief, and the costs memorandum is due 14 days after the opinion.","c":"I.R.C.P. 54(d)(4)-(5), (e)(5); I.A.R. 40(c), 41(a), (d)"},{"t":"No automatic 30-day stay of judgment, and only 14 days after appeal","d":"Unlike the federal rule, Idaho has no automatic stay after entry of judgment; execution may issue at once unless the court orders otherwise. Filing a civil notice of appeal stays proceedings and execution for only 14 days. A stay of a money judgment needs a bond or cash deposit of the judgment plus 36%.","c":"I.R.C.P. 62(a); I.A.R. 13(a), 13(b)(15)"},{"t":"Motion practice runs on a 14/7/2-day schedule tied to the hearing","d":"A motion, supporting affidavits and briefs, and the notice of hearing must be filed and served so they are received at least 14 days before the hearing. Opposition is due at least 7 days before, and a reply at least 2 days before. The motion must say whether oral argument is wanted and whether a brief will be filed. Summary judgment instead runs 28/14/7 days before the hearing, and the motion must be filed at least 90 days before trial.","c":"I.R.C.P. 7(b)(3), 56(b)"},{"t":"Do not plead punitive damages","d":"A complaint with a prayer for punitive damages violates the statute. Move to amend later, after showing at a hearing a reasonable likelihood of proving punitive damages at trial.","c":"Idaho Code § 6-1604(2)"},{"t":"Fee demand letters before suing on small claims","d":"To recover fees in actions pleading $35,000 or less, the plaintiff must send a written demand at least 10 days before filing. For personal injury, the demand and an itemized statement of claim with records must reach the insurer (or the defendant) at least 60 days before filing, and new injuries or damage items later waive fees.","c":"Idaho Code § 12-120(1), (4)"},{"t":"Do not contact the other side’s retained expert","d":"Idaho’s discovery rule bars contacting a retained expert disclosed by another party without that party’s permission or a court order. Discovery scope still uses “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence,” and the text contains no mandatory initial-disclosure requirement.","c":"I.R.C.P. 26(b)(1), 26(b)(4)"},{"t":"Every caption needs an Idaho Bar number, and appellate papers need an Idaho signer","d":"The attorney of record’s name, address, phone, email, and current Idaho State Bar number must appear above the court’s title on the first page. In the appellate courts, at least one licensed Idaho attorney of record must sign every notice, motion, petition, and brief. Pro hac vice local counsel must attend all court proceedings unless excused.","c":"I.R.C.P. 2(a)(4); I.A.R. 11.2(a); I.B.C.R. 227(b)(2)"},{"t":"Rule 60(b) has a 6-month outer limit","d":"A motion for relief from judgment for mistake, excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, or fraud must be made within a reasonable time and no more than 6 months after entry of the judgment. The federal rule allows up to one year.","c":"I.R.C.P. 60(c)(1)"}],"judge":[{"t":"Disqualification without cause: one per party, short deadline","d":"In every civil action and petition for judicial review, each party may file one motion to disqualify the judge without stating grounds. The deadline is whichever comes first: 7 days after service of a notice or order setting a status conference, pretrial conference, trial, or the first contested motion hearing, or 21 days after service or receipt of a pleading or order showing who the presiding judge will be. In any event, the motion must be filed before that judge presides over a status conference, pretrial conference, contested proceeding, or trial. Co-parties may be required to share one motion. A party joined later gets 14 days. A newly assigned judge, or a new trial, gives a fresh motion unless the party already used its one. Alternate judges listed in a trial-setting notice may be challenged within 14 days, again only if the motion has not been used. Mail a copy to the judge’s resident chambers.","c":"I.R.C.P. 40(a)(1)-(7)","v":true},{"t":"Where it does not apply","d":"No disqualification without cause of a judge acting in an appellate capacity (unless the appeal is a trial de novo), a post-conviction judge who entered the challenged judgment, or a judge the Supreme Court appointed to a specific civil action. If the motions are used to delay or are filed so often that they impede the administration of justice, the Administrative Director of Courts reviews the misuse.","c":"I.R.C.P. 40(a)(8)-(9)","v":true},{"t":"Disqualification for cause, at any time","d":"A party may move at any time to disqualify a judge who is a party or interested, is related to a party within the third degree, was counsel for a party, or is biased or prejudiced for or against a party or the subject matter. The motion needs an affidavit stating the specific grounds and facts. The challenged judge decides it after notice and hearing. Once a disqualification motion is filed, the judge may act only to grant or deny it. A judge may also step aside without stating a reason.","c":"I.R.C.P. 40(b)-(d)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"UPEPA: broad construction for state and federal speech rights","d":"Idaho’s anti-SLAPP statute protects speech, press, assembly, petition, and association rights “guaranteed by the constitution of the United States or the constitution of the state of Idaho” and directs that it be broadly construed and applied to protect those rights, with an eye to uniformity among enacting states.","c":"Idaho Code §§ 6-3902(2)(c), 6-3911, 6-3912","v":true},{"t":"Whether the Idaho Constitution protects speech more broadly: not checked","d":"The collected texts do not include the Idaho Constitution or case law, so whether Idaho courts read the state free-speech guarantee more broadly than the First Amendment was not verified.","c":"Idaho Const. (not in collected texts)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Statutory fee-shifting is common, and you must ask for it in time","d":"Idaho Code § 12-120 gives the prevailing party attorney’s fees in actions pleading $35,000 or less (after a written demand at least 10 days before suit), in personal injury claims of $35,000 or less (after a demand and statement of claim served at least 60 days before suit), and in any commercial transaction or action on an account, note, or contract for goods or services. Section 12-121 allows fees for a case brought, pursued, or defended frivolously, unreasonably, or without foundation; under Rule 54(e)(2) that requires a written finding and is not available on a default judgment. Claim costs and fees by memorandum within 14 days after entry of judgment or the right is waived.","c":"Idaho Code §§ 12-120(1), (3), (4), 12-121; I.R.C.P. 54(d)(4), (e)(2), (e)(5)","v":true},{"t":"Offer of judgment compares an “adjusted award”","d":"Idaho Rule 68 lets a defending party serve an offer at least 14 days before trial, with 14 days to accept. For money claims, the comparison is to an “adjusted award” (the verdict plus the offeree’s pre-offer costs and fees). If the adjusted award is less than the offer, the offeree pays the offeror’s post-offer costs. That is a different calculation from the federal rule.","c":"I.R.C.P. 68","v":true},{"t":"Punitive damages cannot be pleaded at the outset","d":"No complaint may pray for punitive damages. A party must move to amend before trial and, after a hearing, show a reasonable likelihood of proving facts at trial that support punitive damages. At trial the standard is clear and convincing evidence of oppressive, fraudulent, malicious, or outrageous conduct.","c":"Idaho Code § 6-1604(1)-(2)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Idaho Appellate Rules (I.A.R.), all rules as displayed on isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/iar, saved November 10, 2026, including amendments adopted April 29 and June 30, 2026 (effective July 1, 2026); Idaho Rules of Civil Procedure 2.2, 11.2, 50, 52, 54, 58, 59, 60, 62, 83, 83.1; Idaho Rules for Electronic Filing and Service 12, 17, 20, 21; Idaho Bar Commission Rule 227; Idaho Code § 6-3909 (Uniform Public Expression Protection Act), from legislature.idaho.gov. Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Idaho: 42 days, file in the trial court, and the Supreme Court decides who hears it","d":"Every appeal from a district court is an appeal to the Idaho Supreme Court. File the notice of appeal with the district court clerk within 42 days of the clerk’s filing stamp on the judgment. That deadline is jurisdictional and cannot be extended. The Supreme Court then keeps the case or assigns it to the four-judge Court of Appeals. A Court of Appeals decision can be taken to the Supreme Court only by a discretionary petition for review, filed within 21 days.\nMost civil appeals from a magistrate judge go first to the district judge under I.R.C.P. 83, not to the Supreme Court. Briefing is counted in words (14,000 for a first brief), and attorney fees on appeal are lost unless claimed in your first brief.","c":"I.A.R. 4, 11, 14(a), 21, 34(b), 41(a), 108, 118; I.R.C.P. 83"},"groups":[["Which court",[{"t":"All appeals are filed as Supreme Court appeals","d":"A party aggrieved by an appealable district court judgment or order, or a decision of the Public Utilities Commission or Industrial Commission, appeals to the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court decides which cases to assign to the Court of Appeals. It never assigns original proceedings, capital appeals, Industrial Commission or Public Utilities Commission appeals, or Bar and Judicial Council matters. It ordinarily keeps cases of substantial public interest, first impression, significant constitutional questions, challenges to the validity of statutes or ordinances, and conflicts in the case law. Cases applying existing law generally go to the Court of Appeals. After the notice of appeal, everything is filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court (who is also the Court of Appeals clerk), and the caption and number do not change on assignment.","c":"I.A.R. 4, 11, 101, 106, 108, 110"},{"t":"Magistrate decisions: usually to the district judge first","d":"Final magistrate judgments and listed orders in civil cases are appealed to the district court within 42 days of the file-stamped judgment; a timely motion for JNOV, to amend findings, to alter or amend, or for new trial restarts the time. The district court’s decision can then go to the Supreme Court as of right. Direct appeals from a magistrate to the Supreme Court are limited to final judgments on termination of parental rights or adoption (as of right) and, by permission, custody, Child Protective Act, and guardianship matters.","c":"I.R.C.P. 83; I.A.R. 11(a)(2), 11.1, 12.1"},{"t":"Appeal filed in the wrong court can be transferred (new July 1, 2026)","d":"A timely notice of appeal filed in the wrong court (Supreme Court instead of district court, or the reverse) may be transferred, and it is deemed filed in the receiving court on the original filing date. No new filing fee is due except to cure a deficiency.","c":"I.A.R. 11.3"}]],["What is appealable",[{"t":"Appeals as of right in civil cases","d":"Final judgments under I.R.C.P. 54(a); district court decisions on appeals from magistrates; Rule 54(b)-certified partial judgments; contempt orders; orders granting or refusing a new trial; orders on JNOV motions; orders after final judgment (except an order granting a motion to set aside or vacate the judgment); any order made appealable by statute; and vexatious-litigant designations. Attach a copy of the judgment or order to the notice.","c":"I.A.R. 11(a)(1)-(9)"},{"t":"The judgment must be a bare separate document","d":"A “judgment” is a separate document titled “Judgment” or “Decree” that begins “JUDGMENT IS ENTERED AS FOLLOWS:” and states the relief without recitals, reasoning, or findings. It is final only if it resolves all claims as to all parties (except costs and fees) or is certified under Rule 54(b). The clerk’s filing stamp is entry.","c":"I.R.C.P. 54(a)-(b), 58; I.A.R. 11(a)(1), (3)"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP denials: appeal as of right by statute","d":"Under the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (claims filed or asserted on or after January 1, 2026), a moving party may appeal as of right from an order denying the special motion in whole or part, within 42 days after entry of the order. Proceedings between all parties are stayed during the appeal. The Appellate Rules do not mention the Act; rely on I.A.R. 11(a)(8) and cite § 6-3909 in the notice’s jurisdictional statement.","c":"Idaho Code §§ 6-3904(3), 6-3909; I.A.R. 11(a)(8), 17(g)"}]],["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal: 42 days from the file stamp, filed in the trial court","d":"File the notice with the clerk of the district court within 42 days from the clerk’s filing stamp on the judgment or order. Electronic filing under the Idaho Rules for Electronic Filing and Service counts as physical filing, but the notice is not filed until received; mailing is not enough. Late filing is jurisdictional, causes automatic dismissal, and cannot be cured by extension. A premature notice becomes valid when the judgment is file-stamped.","c":"I.A.R. 14(a), 17(e)(2), 20, 21, 44, 46"},{"t":"Contents, fees, and transcript designation","d":"The notice must include the parties and counsel (with email addresses), the judgment appealed (copy attached), a preliminary statement of issues, a jurisdictional statement, designations of the transcript and any added record items and exhibits, and certifications of service on the reporter and payment of the estimated transcript, record, and filing fees. The notice brings up earlier interlocutory orders. Filing fee: $94 (civil appeal or cross-appeal). There is no standard transcript in civil appeals; request specific proceedings by reporter, date, title, and page estimate, in electronic format. No cost bond is required.","c":"I.A.R. 16(a), 17, 23(a), 25(c); I.R.E.F.S. 20(a)"},{"t":"Cross-appeal","d":"File a notice of cross-appeal in the district court within the 42-day period for the judgment, or within 21 days after the original notice of appeal was filed, whichever is later. It is also jurisdictional. A respondent seeking no affirmative relief may raise additional issues in its brief without cross-appealing.","c":"I.A.R. 11(g), 15, 21, 35(b)(4)"},{"t":"Record and transcript","d":"The clerk’s record is due within 28 days after the notice is filed. A transcript estimated under 100 pages is due 30 days after the reporter is notified, and 100 to 500 pages in 63 days. Respondents may request additions within 14 days. After service of the record and transcript, parties have 28 days to object, or they are settled. The clerk then files them with the Supreme Court within 7 days, and briefing runs from that filing. Augmentation is by motion to the Supreme Court any time before the opinion.","c":"I.A.R. 19, 24(e), 27(d), 29, 30; I.R.E.F.S. 20(b)-(e)"}]],["Tolling and post-judgment motions",[{"t":"What restarts the clock","d":"A timely motion that, if granted, could affect any findings, conclusions, or the judgment terminates the appeal period; a new 42 days runs from the file stamp on the order deciding it. Rule 60 motions and motions about costs or attorney fees do not.","c":"I.A.R. 14(a)"},{"t":"Post-judgment motions are due in 14 days","d":"JNOV, amended findings, new trial, and motions to alter or amend are each due within 14 days after entry of judgment. A motion to reconsider an interlocutory order may be filed any time before, or within 14 days after, final judgment. The trial court cannot extend these times. A Rule 60(b) motion for mistake, new evidence, or fraud has a 6-month outer limit.","c":"I.R.C.P. 2.2(b)(3), 11.2(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 60(c)(1)"},{"t":"Trial court keeps limited jurisdiction during the appeal","d":"While the appeal is pending, the district court may still decide post-trial and Rule 60 motions, tax trial costs and fees, set stays and bonds, issue Rule 54(b) certificates, and settle the transcript.","c":"I.A.R. 13(b)"}]],["Stays",[{"t":"Only a 14-day automatic stay","d":"Idaho has no automatic stay on entry of judgment; enforcement may issue at once unless the court orders otherwise. Filing a civil notice of appeal or cross-appeal stays proceedings and execution for 14 days (not civil protection orders). Any further stay requires an order of the district court or Supreme Court, and you must apply to the district court first.","c":"I.R.C.P. 62(a); I.A.R. 13(a), 13(g)"},{"t":"Money judgments: judgment plus 36%","d":"A money judgment is stayed by a cash deposit or surety bond equal to the judgment plus 36%, or by a written agreement not to execute. Objections to bond sufficiency are waived unless made by motion within 21 days. The bond requirement is waived for punitive damages above $1,000,000 and may be waived for good cause. Other stays require a likelihood of success and of irreparable harm.","c":"I.A.R. 13(b)(8), (14), (15), 13(g)(1), 16(b)"}]],["Briefing",[{"t":"Due dates","d":"Appellant: 35 days after the transcript and record are filed with the Supreme Court. Respondent (and cross-appellant, in a joint brief): 28 days after service of the appellant’s brief. Cross-respondent: 28 days after the cross-appellant’s brief. Reply: 21 days after service of the respondent’s brief. The Clerk notifies counsel of the due dates. Extensions are disfavored and must be sought by the due date with an affidavit covering eight listed items.","c":"I.A.R. 29(b), 34(c)-(d), 46"},{"t":"Word limits and format","d":"First brief: 14,000 words; reply: 7,000 words (headings, footnotes, and quotations count; tables, certificates, and appendices do not). A certificate stating the word count goes immediately before the signature. Briefs must be 14-point or larger Times New Roman or Century Schoolbook, double-spaced with 1-inch margins and bottom page numbers. Record citations go in the text, not footnotes. File only the original, electronically; no copies.","c":"I.A.R. 34(a)-(b), 35(e), 36(b); I.R.E.F.S. 20(f)"},{"t":"Fees on appeal must be claimed in your first brief","d":"A party seeking attorney fees on appeal must list the claim and its legal basis among the issues in its first brief, or it is waived unless the Court allows a later claim. If fees are awarded, file the claim with the memorandum of costs within 14 days after the opinion; a late costs memorandum waives costs.","c":"I.A.R. 35(a)(5), (b)(5), 40(c), 41(a), (d)"},{"t":"Oral argument and motions","d":"Oral argument is held in all appeals unless waived by stipulation with Court approval, or unless the Court orders submission on the briefs; an objection to that order is due within 21 days. Each side gets 30 minutes. Responses to motions are due within 14 days. A motion to dismiss for rule violations must be filed at least 21 days before argument; a motion to dismiss for a late notice of appeal may be filed at any time.","c":"I.A.R. 32(a), (d), 37(a)-(b)"}]],["Decision, rehearing, and Supreme Court review",[{"t":"Rehearing: 21 days, brief 14 days later","d":"A petition for rehearing must be physically filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 21 days after the opinion is filed. The filing fee is $71. The supporting brief follows within 14 days after the petition. The deadline is jurisdictional and cannot be extended. The same rule applies to Court of Appeals decisions.","c":"I.A.R. 21, 23(a)(7), 42(a)-(b), 46, 116"},{"t":"Petition for review of a Court of Appeals decision","d":"File within 21 days after the Court of Appeals opinion or order, after an order denying rehearing, or after an opinion on rehearing. A supporting brief limited to the review criteria is due with the petition or within 14 days. There is no fee. Review is discretionary and needs a majority of the justices, based on factors like an unsettled question, conflict with precedent, or a departure from usual procedure. The Court may also order review on its own within 21 days.","c":"I.A.R. 23(a)(11), 118, 120"},{"t":"Finality and remittitur","d":"Opinions become final 21 days after the latest announcement of the opinion, an opinion on rehearing, or a modified opinion; the Clerk then issues the remittitur. A surety must pay within 30 days after the remittitur is filed.","c":"I.A.R. 38, 122, 13(b)(15)"}]],["Interlocutory appeals",[{"t":"Permissive appeal (I.A.R. 12)","d":"For an interlocutory district court order involving a controlling question of law with substantial grounds for difference of opinion, where an immediate appeal may materially advance the litigation: move in the district court within 14 days after entry of the order. The court rules within 14 days after the hearing. Any party may then move in the Supreme Court within 14 days after that ruling, or if the court has not ruled within 21 days after the motion was filed. The appeal exists only if the Supreme Court accepts it and sets a time for the notice of appeal. A motion for permission does not stay the case.","c":"I.A.R. 12, 13(f), 13.4"},{"t":"Magistrate interlocutory orders go to the district court","d":"A parallel procedure (14 days to the magistrate, then 14 days to the district court) applies to interlocutory magistrate orders, and a district court denial is final.","c":"I.R.C.P. 83.1"}]],["Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel",[{"t":"Electronic filing","d":"Briefs, original petitions, motions, and memoranda of costs must be filed electronically with the Supreme Court. Transcripts and records are electronic. Counsel must provide an email address for service and keep a current email address on file with the Idaho State Bar.","c":"I.R.E.F.S. 20, 21; I.A.R. 47(d)"},{"t":"Out-of-state lawyers","d":"A licensed Idaho attorney must move at least 14 days before argument for an out-of-state lawyer to argue, substantially in the I.B.C.R. 227(j) form. This is unnecessary if the lawyer was already admitted pro hac vice in the case. Every appellate paper must be signed by at least one licensed Idaho attorney of record.","c":"I.A.R. 9, 11.2(a); I.B.C.R. 227"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil, as of right)","d":"42 days from clerk’s filing stamp on the judgment or order.\nJurisdictional; filed in the district court; no extension (I.A.R. 46). Restarts after a timely motion that could affect findings or the judgment (not Rule 60 or costs/fees motions).","c":"I.A.R. 14(a), 21","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s filing stamp on the judgment or order)","note":"Jurisdictional; filed in the district court; no extension (I.A.R. 46). Restarts after a timely motion that could affect findings or the judgment (not Rule 60 or costs/fees motions)."}},{"t":"Notice of cross-appeal","d":"42 days from filing stamp on the judgment, or 21 days after the notice of appeal, whichever is later.\nJurisdictional.","c":"I.A.R. 15(b), 21","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (filing stamp on the judgment, or 21 days after the notice of appeal, whichever is later)","note":"Jurisdictional."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal, order denying anti-SLAPP (UPEPA) motion","d":"42 days from entry of the order denying the § 6-3903 motion in whole or part.\nAppeal of right for the moving party; all proceedings stayed during the appeal (§ 6-3904(3)).","c":"Idaho Code § 6-3909; I.A.R. 11(a)(8)","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order denying the § 6-3903 motion in whole or part)","note":"Appeal of right for the moving party; all proceedings stayed during the appeal (§ 6-3904(3))."}},{"t":"Appeal from magistrate to district court","d":"42 days from file-stamped magistrate judgment or order.\nJurisdictional; cross-appeal within 14 days after service of a notice filed more than 28 days after entry.","c":"I.R.C.P. 83","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (file-stamped magistrate judgment or order)","note":"Jurisdictional; cross-appeal within 14 days after service of a notice filed more than 28 days after entry."}},{"t":"Motion for new trial, to alter or amend, to amend findings, or JNOV","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (I.R.C.P. 2.2(b)(3)).","c":"I.R.C.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended (I.R.C.P. 2.2(b)(3))."}},{"t":"Motion for permission to appeal (district court)","d":"14 days from entry of the interlocutory order.\nThen 14 days from the ruling (or after 21 days with no ruling) to move in the Supreme Court (I.A.R. 12(c)).","c":"I.A.R. 12(b)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the interlocutory order)","note":"Then 14 days from the ruling (or after 21 days with no ruling) to move in the Supreme Court (I.A.R. 12(c))."}},{"t":"Automatic stay after notice of appeal","d":"14 days from filing of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal.\nFurther stays only by court order; apply to district court first.","c":"I.A.R. 13(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal or cross-appeal)","note":"Further stays only by court order; apply to district court first."}},{"t":"Objection to supersedeas bond","d":"21 days from filing of the bond or cash deposit.\nWaived if not made by motion in time; bond is judgment plus 36%.","c":"I.A.R. 13(b)(15)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the bond or cash deposit)","note":"Waived if not made by motion in time; bond is judgment plus 36%."}},{"t":"Respondent’s request for added transcript or record","d":"14 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or amended notice).","c":"I.A.R. 19","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Objections to transcript and record","d":"28 days from service of the transcript and record.\nSettled if no objection.","c":"I.A.R. 29(a)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (service of the transcript and record)","note":"Settled if no objection."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"35 days from filing of the transcript and record with the Supreme Court.\n14,000 words.","c":"I.A.R. 34(c)","due":{"n":35,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the transcript and record with the Supreme Court)","note":"14,000 words."}},{"t":"Respondent’s brief","d":"28 days from service of appellant’s brief.\nCombined with any cross-appellant’s brief.","c":"I.A.R. 34(c)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (service of appellant’s brief)","note":"Combined with any cross-appellant’s brief."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"21 days from service of respondent’s brief.\n7,000 words.","c":"I.A.R. 34(c)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (service of respondent’s brief)","note":"7,000 words."}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"14 days from service of the motion.","c":"I.A.R. 32(d)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":""}},{"t":"Out-of-state counsel motion to argue","d":"14 days from before the hearing or argument (at least).\nNot needed if already admitted pro hac vice in the case.","c":"I.A.R. 9","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (before the hearing or argument)","note":"Not needed if already admitted pro hac vice in the case."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"21 days from filing of the opinion.\nJurisdictional; $71 fee; supporting brief within 14 days after the petition.","c":"I.A.R. 42(a)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the opinion)","note":"Jurisdictional; $71 fee; supporting brief within 14 days after the petition."}},{"t":"Petition for review (Court of Appeals decision)","d":"21 days from announcement of the opinion, order denying rehearing, or opinion on rehearing.\nBrief with the petition or within 14 days; no fee.","c":"I.A.R. 118(a)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (announcement of the opinion, order denying rehearing, or opinion on rehearing)","note":"Brief with the petition or within 14 days; no fee."}},{"t":"Memorandum of costs and attorney fee claim on appeal","d":"14 days from filing and announcement of the opinion.\nLate memorandum waives costs; objections within 14 days of service.","c":"I.A.R. 40(c), 41(d)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing and announcement of the opinion)","note":"Late memorandum waives costs; objections within 14 days of service."}},{"t":"Opinion final; remittitur issues","d":"21 days from last announcement of the opinion (or opinion on rehearing or modified opinion).","c":"I.A.R. 38(b)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (last announcement of the opinion)","note":""}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://isc.idaho.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Idaho Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules; district court rules","s":"The rules apply uniformly in district courts and magistrate’s divisions in all civil actions, proceedings and appeals, except small claims (governed only as the Small Claims Rules provide), certain mental-hospitalization and neurocognitive-treatment proceedings (governed by ICAR) and family law proceedings (governed by the IRFLP); they are to be construed to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of every action. Each judicial district’s judges may make internal case-management rules by majority vote of all district judges, but those rules must be consistent with these rules and approved and published by Supreme Court order before taking effect (unless the Supreme Court declares an emergency). Amended effective October 1, 2024.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"1.1","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The rules do not extend or limit the jurisdiction of any Idaho court or the venue of actions.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"1.2","t":"Jurisdictional amounts for assignment to magistrates; counterclaims and cross-claims","s":"The jurisdictional amounts in Idaho Code sections 1-2208 and 1-2210 or the ICAR exclude interest, costs, punitive damages and attorney fees, and the amounts of all counts are added together, though the assigned judge may decide by written order the actual amount in controversy where several counts seek the same or similar relief. If a counterclaim or cross-claim filed in the magistrate’s division exceeds the magistrate’s jurisdiction, the whole action and the claim must be transferred to a district judge.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"1.3","t":"Objection to assignment to magistrates","s":"Any irregularity in assigning a case to a magistrate, and any objection to the propriety of the assignment, is waived unless a written objection is filed before the trial or hearing begins. An order or judgment is not void or open to collateral attack because it was rendered under an improper assignment to a magistrate.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"2","t":"Form of documents; caption; name of parties; language; abbreviation; and numbers","s":"Filed documents must be black ink on 8½” by 11” white paper (prisoners and approved forms may be hand-printed), with a caption, the title of the court starting at least 3 inches from the top, and attorney (with Idaho bar number) or self-represented party contact information starting at least 1.2 inches from the top. Body text must be double or 1½ spaced, at least 11-point, with margins of at least 1.2 inches at the top and sides and 1 inch at the bottom (unless slightly smaller margins let the document fit on one page), and the document title must appear at the bottom of every page. Other requirements: exhibits legible, handwritten exhibits accompanied by a machine-printed duplicate, filing fee category and amount stated where a fee applies, the complaint naming all parties (later pleadings may name the first party on each side), pleadings in English, and common abbreviations and numbers as words or numerals allowed.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"2.1","t":"Electronic signatures","s":"An electronic signature may be used on any document transmitted electronically, and a notary’s seal may be in electronic form.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"2.2","t":"Computing and extending time","s":"In computing a period stated in days or longer, exclude the trigger day, count every day including weekends and legal holidays, and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday, in which case the period runs to the next day that is not one; if the clerk’s office is inaccessible, filing time extends to the first accessible day. The court may extend time for good cause, with or without motion if acted on or requested before the time expires, or after expiration on motion showing excusable neglect, and the parties may extend time by written stipulation filed before or after expiration if it does not disturb the orderly dispatch of business or the court’s convenience. The court may not extend the time under Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (d) and (e), and 60(b), and 3 days are added when a party must act within a set time after service by mail.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"2.3","t":"Serving notice of an order or judgment","s":"The prevailing party (or the party the court designates) must serve a proposed order or judgment on each party and give the clerk enough copies and stamped, addressed envelopes for service unless the court orders otherwise. Immediately after entry, the clerk must serve the order or judgment, bearing the filing stamp, on every party by mail, email or delivery and note the mailing in the record; mailing is sufficient notice for all purposes under these rules. Lack of notice does not extend the time to appeal or file a post-judgment motion, except where the record shows no mailing by the clerk and the affected party had no actual notice.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"2.4","t":"Setting hearings by court","s":"The court on its own initiative may notice for hearing any pending motion, trial or proceeding by giving notice to all parties as these rules require.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"2.5","t":"Stipulations not binding on court; continuance of trial or hearing","s":"Parties may stipulate to any procedural matter, including vacating or continuing a hearing or trial, but the stipulation is treated as a joint motion and does not bind the court, which may approve or disapprove it as it would a motion. The court may, by oral or written notice, limit the time within which a motion or stipulation to vacate or continue a hearing or trial must be made.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"2.6","t":"Privacy protection for filings made with the court","s":"The filer is responsible for omitting or redacting personal data identifiers before filing (the clerk does not review), and identifiers should be left out unless required by the court, statute or rule or material to the proceedings. When they must be included, redact to the last four digits for Social Security numbers, financial account numbers, driver’s license and state ID numbers, and employer or taxpayer ID numbers, use only the initials of minor children, and use only the birth year (XX/XX/1998 format) for dates of birth; exceptions cover records filed before the rule’s effective date, documents exempt under ICAR 32, and a minor’s full name in a name change. A filer may submit the redacted document with a reference list or with an unredacted copy (both exempt from disclosure), a filer who inadvertently submits an unredacted document must file a redacted copy as soon as possible and ask that the original be marked exempt, and failure to comply is grounds for contempt and possible sanctions. The rule also tells courts to avoid including identifiers in orders, judgments and decrees and lists other sensitive information (medical, employment, financial, insurance, trade secret, government cooperation, crime victim) on which parties should use caution.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"2.7","t":"Declarations","s":"Where the rules require or permit a statement under oath or affirmation, it may be made as provided in Idaho Code section 9-1406, and an affidavit includes a written certification or declaration made that way.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"2.8","t":"Unsworn foreign declarations","s":"This is Idaho’s version of the Uniform Unsworn Foreign Declarations Act: an unsworn declaration made under penalty of perjury by a declarant physically outside the boundaries of the United States has the same effect as a sworn declaration wherever Idaho law requires or permits one, if it is in substantially the form in Appendix B and in any medium Idaho law requires for sworn declarations. It does not apply to depositions, oaths of office, oaths required before a specified official other than a notary, declarations to be recorded under Idaho Code section 55-805, or oaths required by Idaho Code section 15-2-504, and the rule includes definitions and drafting comments.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"2.9","t":"Lost papers","s":"If an original pleading or paper is lost, the court may authorize a copy to be filed and used instead of the original.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"2.10","t":"Reclaiming exhibits, documents or property","s":"Any party or interested person may apply to the trial court for return of exhibits, documents or property displayed or considered in the action, but only after the latest of the expiration of the time for appeal, the determination of an appeal, or the determination of any proceeding following an appeal and expiration of the time to appeal from that determination. The court may grant the application on appropriate conditions, such as substituting a copy or other reproduction or posting a bond to return the item if later needed.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action","s":"There is one form of action, the civil action, commenced by filing a complaint, petition or application; filing parties are designated plaintiff or petitioner and opposing parties defendant or respondent. Except for governmental entities as defined in Idaho Code section 6-902(3) and the listed case types, every party must file a Supreme Court approved case information sheet (exempt from disclosure) with its first appearance, while in guardianship, conservatorship, adoption, termination of parental rights, involuntary commitment and child protection act cases the initiating party must file it with the complaint or petition.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"4","t":"Summons","s":"The summons must contain the items listed in the rule (court, case number, parties, county, clerk and plaintiff’s attorney contact information, the time to appear and defend, default warning, clerk’s signature and court seal) in substantially the Appendix B form, and the clerk issues one for each defendant on or after filing. If a defendant is not served within 182 days after the complaint is filed, the court, on motion or on its own after 14 days’ notice to the plaintiff, must dismiss without prejudice as to that defendant unless the plaintiff shows good cause, in which case the court must extend the time. The summons and complaint may be served by an officer or any non-party over 18, on individuals (personally, at their abode with a resident at least 18, or on an agent), on a person under 14 or an incompetent person (through a guardian, parent or other specified person, and also on the minor or incompetent person unless the court orders otherwise), on corporations and associations (an officer or agent, or by registered or certified mail to the registered place of business and the president or secretary if the agent is unavailable), on the State (2 copies to the attorney general or an assistant), and on other governmental bodies (their chief executive officer, secretary or clerk). Other provisions cover service outside the state and by publication, completion of service, process served anywhere in the state, and proof of service (officer certificate, affidavit, postal receipts, publisher’s affidavit, or acknowledged written admission), which the court may allow to be amended.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"4.1","t":"General or special appearance","s":"A voluntary appearance or service of a pleading submits the party to personal jurisdiction, except that a Rule 12(b)(2), (4) or (5) motion, a Rule 40(a) or (b) motion, a motion to extend time to answer or appear, joinder of other defenses in such a motion, responding to discovery or motions after filing such a motion, and pleading further after denial of such a motion are not voluntary appearances. A document called a “special appearance” that seeks no relief also preserves the objection if a Rule 12(b)(2), (4) or (5) motion is filed within 14 days after it, or within any later time the court permits.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"5","t":"Serving and filing pleadings and other papers","s":"Orders requiring service, later pleadings, discovery papers, written motions (except ex parte), and notices, appearances, demands and offers of judgment must be served on every party (not on a party in default for failing to appear, unless a new claim is asserted, which must be served under Rule 4), and service on a represented party is made on the attorney. Service may be made by hand, by leaving at the office or abode, by mail (complete on mailing), with the clerk if there is no known address, electronically if the person consented in writing (complete on transmission), by fax, or by other means consented to in writing, and the court may relieve service among numerous defendants. Papers after the complaint must be filed with a certificate of service either before service or within a reasonable time after, and may be filed with the clerk, with a judge who agrees to accept them, or by fax subject to the rule’s conditions, including a 10-page limit unless the county allows more; proof of service states the date, manner and the name and address of the person served, and failure to prove service does not affect validity.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"5.1","t":"Attorney legislators","s":"An attorney serving as a legislator during a general or special session need not appear at any trial or proceeding, and the time to file any paper is extended for 10 days after adjournment, without tolling any statutory limitation period. On a motion supported by affidavit, the court may order ex parte that the attorney-legislator appear or arrange substitute counsel if it finds an emergency, undue prejudice to the party, or irreparable damage, and the order must be served on the attorney by certified mail at the legislature.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"6","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved. Idaho puts time computation in Rule 2.2, not Rule 6.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions and other papers","s":"Only these pleadings are allowed: a complaint, an answer to a complaint, an answer to a counterclaim, an answer to a cross claim, a third party complaint, an answer to a third party complaint, and a reply to an answer if the court orders one. A motion must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state its grounds with particularity including the rule number, and state the relief sought; a proposed order must be a separate document. The motion, supporting affidavits and briefs, and any notice of hearing must be filed and served so as to be received at least 14 days before the hearing; opposing affidavits and briefs at least 7 days before; and a reply brief at least 2 days before. The motion must say on its face whether oral argument is desired and whether a brief will be filed, the court may deny a motion without notice if oral argument is not requested or a supporting brief is not timely filed and the motion lacks merit, the court may deny oral argument on at least 1 day’s notice, and a copy must go to a presiding judge located outside the county. The court may change these times for good cause, and they do not apply where a statute or other rule sets a different time.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"7.1","t":"Evidence on a motion","s":"When a motion relies on facts outside the record, the court may hear it on affidavits or wholly or partly on oral testimony or depositions.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"7.2","t":"Hearings by telephone or video teleconference","s":"The court may hear any motion by phone or video, except summary judgment motions unless the parties stipulate; evidentiary hearings by phone or video only when no oral testimony is taken (video testimony allowed if the parties stipulate); and any other pretrial matter. Minutes must be filed and the hearing audio recorded, and a prevailing party may recover the conferencing cost as a discretionary cost.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"7.3","t":"Video teleconferencing for mental commitment hearings","s":"Initial and continuing involuntary commitment hearings may be conducted by video teleconference if the proposed patient is visible and audible to the court and those in the courtroom, can consult privately with counsel if represented, and the court, patient, counsel and any testifying witness can all see, hear and communicate with each other simultaneously. The audio must be recorded and minutes of the hearing prepared and filed in the action.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim for relief must contain a short and plain statement of the grounds for jurisdiction (unless already established), a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief, and a demand for relief, which may be alternative or of different types. A responding party must state its defenses and admit or deny each allegation (by general denial, specific denial, or denial of all except those admitted, with a statement of lack of knowledge having the effect of a denial), and an allegation other than damages is admitted if a required response does not deny it. Affirmative defenses must be affirmatively stated (the rule lists 19, from accord and satisfaction to discharge in bankruptcy); a mistaken designation of a defense or counterclaim is treated as correct if justice requires, and alternative and inconsistent claims or defenses are allowed. Pleadings must be simple, concise and direct, and construed to do justice.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"A pleading need not allege capacity to sue or be sued, representative authority or legal existence of an association unless needed to show jurisdiction, and these issues are raised by specific denial; fraud, mistake and violation of civil or constitutional rights must be pleaded with particularity, while malice, intent and knowledge may be alleged generally. Conditions precedent may be alleged generally but must be denied with particularity, and the statute of limitations may be pleaded generally if the statute or session law relied on is pleaded with particularity. Special damages must be stated by category (dollar amounts may be alleged), but a pleading must not state a dollar amount for general or punitive damages except that it may state that the claim meets a jurisdictional threshold. The rule also covers unknown owners and heirs, official documents, judgments, time and place, libel and slander, and describing real property.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Every pleading must comply with Rule 2 and carry a Rule 7(a) designation, state claims and defenses in numbered paragraphs each limited to a single set of circumstances, and put each claim on a separate transaction or occurrence, and each defense other than a denial, in a separate count or defense where that promotes clarity. Statements may be adopted by reference, a written instrument attached as an exhibit is part of the pleading, and a party whose true name is unknown may be designated as such and the pleading amended when it is discovered.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"10.1","t":"Filing fee waiver","s":"The filing fee in Appendix A must be paid before filing a pleading or motion listed in the fee schedule. The court may waive it on a party’s verified application (no fee for the application), and the fee is automatically waived where the party is represented by a volunteer attorney through the Idaho Law Foundation Volunteer Lawyers Program, the University of Idaho Legal Aid Clinic, the Idaho Legal Aid Program, or a private attorney under contract with Legal Aid. Amended effective April 23, 2026.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing pleadings, motions, and other papers; representations to the court; sanctions","s":"Every paper must be signed by at least one Idaho-licensed attorney of record (or by an unrepresented party) with the signer’s address, email and phone number, pleadings need not be verified unless a rule or statute says so, and the court must strike an unsigned paper unless the omission is promptly corrected. By presenting a paper, the signer certifies after reasonable inquiry that it is not for an improper purpose and that its claims, defenses, factual contentions and denials are warranted. A sanctions motion must be made separately, describe the conduct, and be served under Rule 5 but not filed if the challenged paper is withdrawn or corrected within 21 days after service (or other time the court sets); the court may also act on its own, and may impose monetary or nonmonetary sanctions and refer a person to the administrative district judge for a vexatious litigant determination under ICAR 59, and the sanctions order must describe the conduct and explain the basis. The rule does not apply to discovery (Rules 26 through 37).","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"11.1","t":"Verification","s":"A verification of a pleading must be a written statement or declaration by a party or the party’s attorney of record, sworn or affirmed before an officer authorized under Rule 28 or complying with Idaho Code section 9-1406, that the affiant believes the facts stated are true, unless verification on personal knowledge is required. For a corporation an officer may verify, and for a partnership or unincorporated association a member or officer may verify.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"11.2","t":"Successive applications for orders or writs; motions for reconsideration","s":"If an application for an order or writ is denied in whole or in part, neither the party nor the attorney may apply to another judge except by appeal; an order obtained in violation must be immediately vacated by the issuing judge and the court must sanction the party and attorney, including costs and attorney fees. A second application for a constitutional writ is allowed if the first application and ruling are disclosed to the second judge, and an application may be renewed to the same or a succeeding judge, though that does not itself create a right to reconsideration. A motion to reconsider an interlocutory order may be made at any time before or within 14 days after entry of final judgment, a motion to reconsider an order entered after final judgment must be made within 14 days after entry of the order, and orders on motions under Rules 50(a), 52(b), 55(c), 59(a), 59(e), 59.1, 60(a) or 60(b) cannot be reconsidered.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"11.3","t":"Substitution and withdrawal of attorneys","s":"An attorney may be substituted by filing written notice signed by both the new and withdrawing attorney, and substitution must not delay proceedings except for good cause. Otherwise withdrawal requires leave of court on a noticed motion set for hearing and giving the client’s last known address; the court may grant it for good cause on conditions that prevent delay or prejudice, and after final judgment the attorney may file a notice of withdrawal without leave, effective once the appeal time has expired and nothing is pending. The clerk serves the order allowing withdrawal on all parties, the order must warn the party that claims may be dismissed with prejudice or default entered unless, within 21 days after service, the party appoints new counsel or files notice of self-representation, and the action is stayed for those 21 days; after that the court may dismiss with prejudice or enter default, and default judgment follows Rule 55(b) without the 3-day written notice of the application. On an attorney’s death, extended illness, absence, suspension or disbarment, the court must stay the action unless the attorney is associated with others in the action, and then enter a withdrawal order under subsection (c). Amended effective July 12, 2024.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"11.4","t":"Limited pro bono appearance","s":"Under Idaho Rule of Professional Conduct 1.2(c), an attorney may give limited unpaid assistance to a self-represented litigant by filing and serving a notice of limited appearance specifying the matters undertaken. The attorney acts only on those matters, service on the attorney is valid only for those proceedings, and when they conclude the attorney files a notice of completion of limited appearance, which ends the appearance without leave of court.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections: when and how presented; motion for judgment on the pleadings; consolidating motions; waiving defenses; hearings before trial","s":"Unless another time is set by rule or statute, an answer is due within 21 days after service of the summons and complaint, an answer to a counterclaim or crossclaim is due within 21 days after service of that pleading, and a reply ordered by the court is due 21 days after service of the order unless the court sets another time; if the court denies a Rule 12 motion or postpones it until trial, or grants a motion for a more definite statement, the responsive pleading is due 14 days after notice of the ruling or service of the more definite statement. Eight defenses may be raised by motion (including lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process or service, failure to state a claim, failure to join a party, and another action pending between the same parties for the same cause); a motion for a more definite statement must be made before the responsive pleading, and noncompliance with an order within 14 days allows the court to strike the pleading. A motion to strike must be made before responding or, if no response is allowed, within 21 days after service. The defenses in (b)(2), (4) and (5) are waived if not raised by motion before a responsive pleading or other motion (other than an extension or Rule 40(a) or (b) motion), venue objections are waived unless a timely Rule 40.1 motion is made, and the rule can be joined with a Rule 4.1 special appearance. Matters outside the pleadings convert a Rule 12(b)(6) or 12(c) motion to one for summary judgment under Rule 56.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaims and crossclaims","s":"A pleading must state as a compulsory counterclaim any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence that does not require adding a party over whom the court lacks jurisdiction, subject to the stated exceptions, and may state any other claim as a permissive counterclaim. A counterclaim may seek more or different relief than the opposing party’s claim, the rules do not expand the right to counterclaim or claim a credit against the State of Idaho or its subdivisions, agencies or officers, and the court may permit a supplemental pleading for a counterclaim that matured or was acquired after an earlier pleading. A pleading may state a crossclaim against a coparty arising from the same transaction or occurrence or relating to the property at issue, including a claim that the coparty is or may be liable to the crossclaimant; Rules 19 and 20 govern joining additional parties, and if separate trials are ordered the court may enter judgment on a counterclaim or crossclaim under Rule 54(b). Subdivision (f) is reserved.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may serve a summons and complaint on a nonparty who is or may be liable to it for all or part of the claim, but needs leave of court by motion if the third-party complaint is filed more than 14 days after serving its original answer. The third-party defendant must assert Rule 12 defenses and Rule 13(a) counterclaims against the third-party plaintiff, may assert permissive counterclaims, crossclaims, the third-party plaintiff’s defenses against the plaintiff, and claims arising from the same transaction, and may bring in its own nonparty; the plaintiff may assert claims against the third-party defendant, and any party may move to strike, sever or separately try the third-party claim. A plaintiff may bring in a third party when a claim is asserted against the plaintiff, to the same extent a defendant could.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as a matter of right within 21 days after serving the pleading or, if a responsive pleading is required, within 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or of a Rule 12(b), (e) or (f) motion, whichever is earlier; otherwise amendment requires the opposing party’s written consent or leave of court, which should be freely given when justice requires. Unless the court orders otherwise, a response to an amended pleading is due within the time remaining to respond to the original or within 14 days after service of the amended pleading, whichever is later. During or after trial, the court may allow amendment over an objection to evidence outside the issues, and issues tried by consent are treated as if pleaded, with amendment allowed at any time even after judgment. Amendments relate back when the limitations law allows, when they arise from the same conduct or occurrence, or when a new party had notice and knew or should have known it would have been sued but for a mistake as to identity within the Rule 4(b)(2) service period (notice to the Idaho attorney general or agency suffices for the State), joining a real party in interest follows Rule 17(a), and the court may permit supplemental pleadings on motion and reasonable notice.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"Within 30 days after an answer or notice of appearance is filed (or within 90 days after the complaint is filed, if a defendant has been served but has not appeared), the court must act to get a scheduling order entered, such as by setting a scheduling conference or requesting trial dates. The order covers trial and pretrial dates, deadlines for joining parties, amending pleadings, dispositive motions, discovery and expert disclosure, ADR, and a special master; trial dates change only for good cause, and the other deadlines by good cause or stipulation plus court approval. If the court does not set the case, a party may request a trial setting after all defendants appear, and any other party must respond within 7 days. The court must hold a final pretrial process at least 30 days before trial, may order exhibit and witness lists (late-disclosed items may be excluded absent good cause), and may sanction noncompliance, including fees under Rule 37(b)(2)(A).","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"17","t":"Plaintiff and defendant; capacity","s":"An action must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though executors, administrators, personal representatives, guardians, bailees, express-trust trustees, parties holding a contract made for another’s benefit, and statutorily authorized parties may sue in their own names. The court may not dismiss for failure to name the real party in interest until, after an objection, a reasonable time has been allowed for ratification, joinder, or substitution. Capacity is set by Idaho law; minors and incompetent persons sue through a representative, next friend, or guardian ad litem, and the court must appoint a guardian ad litem or otherwise protect an unrepresented one. Unknown owners, heirs, or devisees may be named and described as parties in property-related actions (title, quiet title, partition, foreclosure, trusts, specific performance, and similar).","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims","s":"A party asserting a claim, counterclaim, cross-claim, or third-party claim may join as many independent or alternative claims as it has against an opposing party. Contingent claims may be joined, and a plaintiff may, for example, plead a money claim and a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first obtaining a money judgment.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"19","t":"Required joinder of parties","s":"A person subject to service must be joined if the court cannot give complete relief without the person, or if the person claims an interest that disposing of the action could impair or that leaves an existing party at risk of double or inconsistent obligations. The court must order joinder of a required person; if joinder is not feasible, the court weighs listed equity factors (prejudice, ways to lessen it, adequacy of a judgment, and the plaintiff’s remedy if dismissed) to decide whether to proceed or dismiss. A pleading asserting a claim must name any required person who is not joined and state why. The rule is subject to Rule 77 for class actions.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"19.1","t":"Motor vehicle operator","s":"In an action against a vehicle owner under Idaho Code section 49-2417, the operator whose negligence is imputed to the owner must be made a defendant if the operator can be personally served in Idaho. This is an Idaho-specific rule with no federal counterpart.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Persons may join as plaintiffs, or be joined as defendants, if the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series of them and a common question of law or fact will arise. A party need not be interested in all the relief demanded, and the court may enter judgment according to each party’s rights or liabilities. The court may issue protective orders, including separate trials, and may direct final judgment on a claim as provided in Rule 54(b).","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal. On motion or on its own, the court may at any time, on just terms, add or drop a party and may sever any claim against a party.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may join the competing claimants as defendants and require them to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability. A defendant facing similar liability may interplead through a cross-claim or counterclaim. The rule supplements, and does not limit, Rule 20 joinder.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"23","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved. Idaho’s class-action rule is Rule 77, not Rule 23.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"On timely motion, the court must allow intervention by anyone given an unconditional right by an Idaho statute, or who claims an interest in the property or transaction that disposition may impair, unless existing parties adequately represent it. The court may allow permissive intervention for a conditional statutory right, a claim or defense sharing a common question with the main action, or a federal or state agency or officer whose statute, order, or regulation underlies a party’s claim or defense, considering undue delay or prejudice. The motion must be served under Rule 5, state the grounds, and be accompanied by a pleading setting out the claim or defense.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, the court may order substitution on motion by any party or the decedent’s successor or representative; if no motion is made within a reasonable time, the action by or against the decedent may be dismissed. The action continues among the remaining parties if the right survives only to or against them. The substitution motion and notice of hearing go to parties under Rule 5 and to nonparties under Rule 4, in any judicial district. Similar procedures apply to incompetency and transfer of interest, a successor public officer is automatically substituted, and substitution may occur before or after judgment or by the Supreme Court pending appeal.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Discovery methods are depositions, interrogatories, document and ESI production, entry on land, physical and mental examinations, and requests for admission. The scope is any nonprivileged matter relevant to a claim or defense and, for good cause, any matter relevant to the subject matter of the action; Idaho keeps the “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence” language, and the text contains no mandatory initial-disclosure provision. Parties may discover insurance agreements; work product is protected absent substantial need; retained experts must be disclosed by interrogatory answer or court order with opinions, data, exhibits, qualifications including publications within the preceding ten years, compensation, and testimony within the preceding four years, while draft reports and most attorney-expert communications are protected and a party may not contact another party’s retained expert without permission. Protective orders require a good-faith conferral certification; there is a duty to supplement only in the circumstances listed (including witness identities and experts), with exclusion as a sanction; and every discovery request, response, or objection must be signed by counsel with an address and e-mail address, with mandatory sanctions for improper certification.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions to perpetuate testimony","s":"Before suit, a person may file a verified petition in the district court where an expected adverse party resides to depose named persons, and must serve each expected adverse party at least 21 days before the hearing; the court may order service by publication if service cannot be made and must appoint an attorney for unserved persons. If it finds perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justice, the court issues an order and the depositions proceed under the rules. A party may also seek leave to perpetuate testimony while an appeal is pending or may still be taken, and the rule does not limit an independent action to perpetuate testimony.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In Idaho, a deposition is taken before a person authorized to administer oaths; elsewhere in the United States, before an officer authorized by Idaho, federal, or local law, or a person appointed by the court. In a foreign country, it may be taken before a U.S. diplomatic or consular officer, an officer authorized under Idaho or federal law, or a court-appointed person, and a commission may issue. Depositions of armed forces members, spouses, and children may be taken before a military officer, whose certificate is prima facie proof. No deposition may be taken before a party’s relative, employee, or attorney, a person related to or employed by a party’s attorney, or anyone financially interested in the action.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations about discovery procedure","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, the parties may stipulate that a deposition be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in the manner specified, and may modify other discovery procedures. A stipulation extending time for any form of discovery needs court approval if it would interfere with the trial date or if another court order requires approval.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions by oral examination","s":"A party may depose any person without leave, except that leave is needed if the deponent is in prison or if the plaintiff seeks the deposition before 30 days have passed since service on the defendant (unless the defendant has noticed a deposition or sought discovery, or the plaintiff certifies the deponent will leave the district for more than 100 miles from the place of trial or leave the country before then). Reasonable written notice must state the time, place, and recording method; testimony may be recorded by audio or audiovisual means but must also be recorded stenographically, and an organization deponent must designate a witness on described matters. Objections must be concise and nonargumentative, and an instruction not to answer is allowed only to preserve a privilege, enforce a court-ordered limit, or present a motion to terminate or limit. The deponent has 30 days after notice that the transcript is available to review it and sign a statement of changes; the transcript is not filed with the court, and the noticing attorney must retain it until 1 year after final disposition. Amended effective September 9, 2016.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"30.1","t":"Audio-visual recording of depositions","s":"Upon notice, a deposition may be recorded by audio-visual means but must also be recorded stenographically; the noticing party bears the recording and transcription costs, and both records are official. The notice and subpoena must say the deposition will be audio-visually recorded, and the rule sets on-camera opening and closing statements, an on-camera oath, announcement of media units, and a time index. The recording may be used as a stenographic deposition may be, the original may not be altered if edited by court order, the noticing attorney retains the recording and exhibits like a transcript, and reasonable costs may be taxed as costs.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions by written questions","s":"A party may depose any person by written questions without leave, except that leave is required (and must be granted consistent with Rule 26(b)(1)(C)) if the deponent has already been deposed without stipulation or is in prison. The notice and questions go to every party, naming the deponent and the officer. Other parties’ questions are due within set times: cross-questions within 14 days after service of the notice and direct questions, redirect within 7 days after cross-questions, and recross within 7 days after redirect, with the court able to extend or shorten them for good cause. The noticing party delivers the questions to the officer and must notify all parties when the deposition is complete.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"32","t":"Using depositions in court proceedings","s":"A deposition may be used against a party who was present, represented, or given reasonable notice, to the extent admissible under the Idaho Rules of Evidence. Depositions may be used to impeach, an adverse party may use a party’s or its officer’s, director’s, managing agent’s, or designee’s deposition for any purpose, and any deposition may be used if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or outside Idaho, unable to attend, not subpoenable, or exceptional circumstances exist. A deposition cannot be used against a party who received less than 14 days’ notice and promptly moved for a protective order still pending at the deposition. The rule also covers use of partial depositions, earlier-action depositions, and waiver of objections, including written-question objections waived if not served in time or, for recross questions, within 7 days.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Unless otherwise stipulated or court-ordered for good cause, a party may serve no more than 40 interrogatories on any other party, including all discrete subparts. They may be served on the plaintiff after the suit is commenced and on any other party with or after the summons and complaint, and must be answered under oath within 30 days after service (shorter or longer by stipulation or order), with objections stated specifically or waived. A party may answer by specifying business records where the burden is substantially the same. Interrogatories and answers are not filed, the propounding party must keep the originals for 1 year after final disposition and expiration of appeal periods, and a notice of when and on whom they were served must be filed.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"34","t":"Producing documents, electronically stored information, and tangible things, or entering onto land, for inspection and other purposes","s":"A party may request inspection, copying, or testing of documents, ESI, and tangible things, or entry onto land, within the scope of Rule 26(b); requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after the summons and complaint. The request must describe items with reasonable particularity and specify a reasonable time, place, and manner. The response is due in writing within 30 days after service (shorter or longer by stipulation or order), must state for each item that inspection will be permitted or object with reasons, and must state the intended ESI form if none was specified or an objection is made. Requests and responses are not filed; the propounding party keeps the originals for 1 year after final disposition and appeal periods, and must file a notice of when and on whom they were served.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"On motion for good cause and notice to all parties and the person to be examined, the court may order a party whose physical or mental condition (including blood group) is in controversy to be examined by a suitably certified examiner, licensed physician, or qualified mental health professional under Idaho Code section 6-1901, excluding nurses. The order must specify time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner, and the person examined has the right to have a representative of choice present on reasonable notice. On request the moving party must deliver the examiner’s detailed written report plus reports of earlier examinations; requesting the report or deposing the examiner waives privilege as to all examinations of the same condition, and the court may exclude the examiner’s testimony if a report is not provided.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A party may serve written requests to admit facts, application of law to fact, opinions about either, and genuineness of documents (with a copy attached unless already furnished); they may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after the summons and complaint. A matter is admitted unless the responding party serves a signed written answer or objection within 30 days after service (shorter or longer by stipulation or order). Answers must specifically deny or explain, objections must state grounds, and the requester may move to test sufficiency. Admissions are conclusive unless the court permits withdrawal or amendment, and requests and responses are not filed; the propounding party keeps the originals for 1 year after final disposition and appeal periods and must file a notice of service.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to cooperate in discovery; sanctions","s":"A party may move to compel disclosure or discovery after certifying a good-faith attempt to confer; evasive or incomplete responses count as failures, and the court must award reasonable expenses including attorney’s fees to the prevailing side on a motion unless the motion was premature, the opposition was substantially justified, or an award would be unjust. For violating a discovery order, the court may establish facts, bar claims or evidence, strike pleadings, stay, dismiss, enter default, or hold in contempt (except for refusing a physical or mental examination), and must order expenses unless substantially justified. A party that fails to supplement or comply with a Rule 16 disclosure order may not use that information or witness unless the failure was substantially justified or harmless, and a party that fails to admit may owe the cost of proving the matter. Sanctions are available for failing to attend one’s deposition or respond to interrogatories or production requests (with a conferral certification), but not for ESI lost through routine good-faith system operation absent exceptional circumstances; subdivision (f) adds a general catch-all for violating orders.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"37.1","t":"Mediation of civil cases","s":"All civil cases may be referred to mediation, in the court’s discretion on a party’s motion, at a Rule 16 conference, on a Rule 16(b) trial-setting request if all parties agree, or at any other time on 7 days’ notice. The parties must select a mediator within 28 days of the mediation order or the court will appoint one from the district’s list, and the first session must occur within 42 days after the parties report their selection or the mediator is appointed, unless the court orders otherwise. The mediator or the parties must report to the court within 7 days after the last session whether the case settled in whole or in part. Trial attorneys and parties or insurers with settlement authority must attend unless excused, mediator fees are shared pro rata unless otherwise arranged, and Idaho Rules of Evidence 408 and 507 protect confidentiality. Amended effective March 28, 2025.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial","s":"A party may demand a jury on any issue triable of right by a jury by serving a written demand (which may be in a pleading) no later than 14 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue, and filing it under Rule 5(d). The demand must state whether the party will stipulate to a jury of fewer than 12 but at least 6. If a party demands a jury on only some issues, any other party may demand a jury on other issues within 14 days after service of the demand (or a shorter time the court orders); a demand not properly served and filed waives jury trial, and it can be withdrawn only with the parties’ consent.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Once a jury is demanded under Rule 38, the action is designated a jury action and all demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate to a nonjury trial or the court finds there is no jury right on those issues. Issues without a proper demand are tried by the court, though the court may order a jury on motion; the court may also use an advisory jury or, with the parties’ consent, a binding jury in actions not triable of right by jury.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"40","t":"Disqualification","s":"Disqualification without cause (40(a)): in all civil actions and petitions for judicial review, each party may file one motion to disqualify the judge without stating grounds. The motion is due the earlier of 7 days after service of a notice or order setting a status conference, pretrial conference, trial, or the first contested motion hearing, or 21 days after service or receipt of a pleading or order showing who the presiding judge will be, and in any event before that judge has presided over a status conference, pretrial conference, contested proceeding, or trial. Co-parties may be required to join in one motion if their interests are sufficiently common; a newly joined party has 14 days from its first appearance or first responsive pleading due date, whichever is first; each party gets one motion as to a new judge or after a new trial is ordered (within the same timing), but not if it already used its one motion; and a party may file one motion against an alternate judge within 14 days after service of notice listing alternates, unless it already used its motion. The motion must be mailed to the judge at resident chambers, and the right does not apply to judges sitting in an appellate capacity (unless trial de novo), post-conviction judges who entered the challenged judgment, or judges appointed by the Supreme Court to a specific action; misuse can lead to Supreme Court restrictions. Disqualification for cause (40(b)) may be made at any time on the grounds listed (party or interested, related within the third degree, former counsel, or bias or prejudice), requires an affidavit of the party or attorney stating specific grounds and facts, and is decided by the challenged judge after notice and hearing. A judge may also disqualify voluntarily without stating a reason, and once a motion is filed the judge may act only to grant or deny it, after which the administrative judge appoints a replacement. Amended effective July 1, 2024.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"40.1","t":"Change of venue","s":"A judge may change venue only on a party’s motion. Venue may be changed in the court’s discretion by statute or on affidavit or other proof that an impartial trial cannot be had in the county or that witness convenience and the ends of justice would be promoted; it must be changed if the county designated in the complaint is improper, on a motion made no later than 14 days after the moving party files a responsive pleading (the court may deny it on discretionary grounds and may sanction the filer if the action was filed in the wrong venue without good cause). The rule also sets out how the case is assigned and reassigned when venue changes within the same judicial district or to a different one, including after a disqualification.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by filing a notice of dismissal before the opposing party serves an answer or a summary judgment motion, or a stipulation signed by all appearing parties; the dismissal is without prejudice unless stated otherwise, but a notice operates as a merits adjudication if the plaintiff previously dismissed a state or federal action on the same claim. Otherwise dismissal at the plaintiff’s request requires a court order, and a defendant may move to dismiss for failure to prosecute or comply with the rules or an order. Involuntary dismissals generally operate as adjudications on the merits, except for lack of jurisdiction or failure to join a Rule 19 party. Under 41(e), any action, appeal, or proceeding (except guardianships, conservatorships, and probate proceedings) with no action for 90 days may be dismissed absent good cause, with prejudice for appeals and otherwise without prejudice, and the clerk must give at least 14 days’ notice before dismissal.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"If actions before the court share a common question of law or fact, the court may join them for hearing or trial, consolidate them, or issue other orders to avoid unnecessary cost or delay. For convenience, to avoid prejudice, or to expedite, the court may order separate trials of issues or claims, preserving any jury right.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"42.1","t":"Trials and hearings in open court; where held","s":"Trials on the merits must be in open court (witnesses may be excluded as the Idaho Rules of Evidence allow) and, so far as convenient, in a regular courtroom, though all trials, hearings, judgments, and orders are deemed done in open court wherever held. A hearing other than a trial or evidentiary hearing may be held outside the county where the action was filed or transferred; a trial or evidentiary hearing may be held outside that county only by stipulation of the parties.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking testimony","s":"Trial testimony is taken in open court unless a statute or rule provides otherwise, though for good cause in compelling circumstances and with safeguards the court may allow contemporaneous transmission from another location; an affirmation may replace an oath. A party needing an interpreter under Idaho Court Administrative Rule 52 for itself or a witness must notify the court at least 14 days before the proceeding (or as soon as practicable for an expedited hearing), and may be charged costs if a failure to do so without good cause causes a postponement. The rule also covers direct and cross-examination order, reexamination and recalling witnesses (leave of court required), views of premises by the court or jury, and inspection by any party of a writing shown to a witness.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"44","t":"Judicial notice of facts and foreign law","s":"The court must take judicial notice as provided by law, and when it notices an adjudicative fact it instructs the jury under Idaho Rule of Evidence 201. A party who wants the court to notice another state’s statutes or laws must submit a memorandum citing the foreign law to the court and opposing counsel at least 14 days before the trial or hearing (the court may deny the request if not submitted), and opposing counsel may reply within 7 days after service of the memorandum.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoenas","s":"A subpoena must be substantially in the Appendix B form, issue from the court where the action is pending, and be issued by the clerk on request or signed by an Idaho-licensed attorney. It may be served by anyone at least 18 years old who is not a party, with fees for 1 day’s attendance and mileage tendered if requested (not required for State subpoenas), and a party can be served through its attorney of record for a hearing or trial. A subpoena for a party to attend a deposition and produce documents must follow Rule 34 and allow at least 30 days to comply; a document subpoena to a nonparty may be served once all parties have appeared or defaulted, after a copy is served on the opposing party at least 7 days before service on the nonparty (unless the court specifies otherwise). The rule also covers motions to quash or modify, responding to subpoenas (including electronically stored information and privilege claims), place of deposition, witness fees under Rule 54(d)(1), contempt for disobedience, and, in 45(j), Idaho’s version of the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act for out-of-state cases (clerk issues an Idaho subpoena from the foreign one, with no file or fee, and motions to quash or modify are brought by petition in the county where discovery occurs).","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"46","t":"Objecting to a ruling or order","s":"A formal exception to a ruling or order is unnecessary; when the ruling is requested or made, a party need only state the action it wants the court to take, or objects to, and the grounds. Failing to object does not prejudice a party who had no opportunity to do so when the ruling was made.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"47","t":"Jury procedure","s":"Juror questionnaire answers are confidential and released only by court order, though the clerk may give copies to attorneys or pro se parties for trial preparation. The rule covers roll call, the oath to the panel, random selection, optional opening statements before voir dire, voir dire (conducted first by the court, then plaintiff, defendant, and other parties), and a struck-jury option. Challenges for cause are unlimited and are made as jurors are questioned (later only for good cause); each party has 4 peremptory challenges (the court may allocate or apportion them among co-parties), and if 1 or 2 additional jurors are seated each party gets 1 extra peremptory challenge, while more than 2 additional jurors gives each party 2. Jurors may take notes, use notebooks, and submit written questions for witnesses at the court’s direction. In actions that may be assigned to the magistrate division the jury is not more than 6; otherwise it is 12 or any smaller number the parties agree to in open court; a 3/4 majority may return a verdict (if more than 1/4 disagree on polling, the jury returns to deliberate), and jury costs may not be taxed as costs.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"48","t":"Mistrial","s":"After trial begins and before a verdict, the court may declare a mistrial on its own motion or a party’s motion if an occurrence at trial has prevented a fair trial. If deliberate misconduct of a party or attorney caused the mistrial, the court may require that party, attorney, or both to pay the opposing parties’ reasonable expenses, including attorney fees.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"49","t":"Verdicts","s":"The court may require a special verdict (written findings on each fact issue, by written questions, forms, or other method), with instructions sufficient for the jury to answer. A party waives a jury on any fact issue not submitted unless it demands submission before the jury retires; if the court makes no finding it is deemed to have found consistently with its judgment. The court may instead use a general verdict with written answers to questions; if the answers are consistent with the verdict judgment is entered, and if answers conflict with the verdict the court may enter judgment on the answers, return the jury for further consideration, or order a new trial (if the answers are also inconsistent with each other, judgment must not be entered).","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"50","t":"Dispositive trial motions","s":"A motion for a directed verdict must state specific grounds; making one at the close of an opponent’s evidence does not require reserving the right to present evidence, and an unsuccessful motion does not waive a jury even if all parties move. A motion for judgment notwithstanding the verdict must be served within 14 days after entry of judgment (or within 14 days after discharge of the jury if no verdict was returned), whether or not a directed verdict was sought, and may be joined with a new trial motion under Rule 59(a). If the court grants a JNOV it must conditionally rule on a new trial, and the party whose verdict was set aside may serve a new trial motion within 14 days after entry of judgment; failing to move for a new trial there waives it.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"51","t":"Jury instructions","s":"Any party may file written requested instructions no later than 7 days before a jury trial begins, and they must be served on and received by all parties at least 7 days before trial; the court need not consider late requests, but may allow requests up to the close of the evidence for matters that could not have been anticipated or were overlooked. An original and a copy are submitted, with the legal citation only on the copy, which is numbered and bears blanks for the court’s ruling. The court should use the latest Idaho Jury Instructions (IDJI) instruction unless another states the law more adequately, may give preliminary instructions (after an instruction conference held before trial), must give copies of instructions to all parties and rule on requests at the close of evidence, and must read the written instructions before closing arguments and send the original plus at least two copies to the jury. A party may not assign error to an instruction unless it objects before the jury deliberates, stating the instruction and grounds.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings and conclusions by the court","s":"In an action tried without a jury or with an advisory jury, the court must find the facts specially and state conclusions of law separately, on the record or in an opinion or memorandum, and judgment is entered under Rule 58; findings are also required for granting or refusing an interlocutory injunction. Findings are not required for default judgments or when ruling on show cause hearings, Rule 12 or 56 motions, or (unless the rules provide otherwise) other motions. Findings are not set aside unless clearly erroneous, and on a party’s motion filed no later than 14 days after entry of judgment the court may amend or add findings (the motion may accompany a Rule 59 motion). A party may not assign error to a lack of findings unless it raised the issue by appropriate motion.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"A court may appoint a master (including a referee, commissioner, auditor, or examiner), but in a jury trial only if the issues are complicated, and in a nonjury trial only for an accounting or on a showing of an exceptional condition. Any party may move to disqualify a master within 14 days after receiving notice of the appointment, and the master must hold the first meeting within 21 days after the appointing order unless the order says otherwise. In nonjury actions the court accepts the master’s findings of fact unless clearly erroneous, and any party may file written objections within 14 days after being served with notice of the filed report; in jury actions the master’s findings are admissible as evidence. The rule also covers compensation, the master’s powers, witnesses, accounting statements, and draft reports.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"A judgment is a separate document titled “Judgment” or “Decree” beginning with the words “JUDGMENT IS ENTERED AS FOLLOWS:” and stating the relief without recitals, reasoning, or findings; it is final only if entered on all claims except costs and fees as to all parties, or if it is a partial judgment certified final under Rule 54(b) on an express finding of no just reason for delay. Costs as a matter of right to the prevailing party include filing and service fees, witness fees of $20.00 per day, travel at $.30 per mile one way, exhibit preparation up to $500 per party, expert fees up to $2,000 per expert, and deposition reporting and one copy; additional discretionary costs require a showing they were necessary, exceptional, and reasonably incurred. A memorandum of costs (and attorney fees, supported by an attorney affidavit) must be filed and served no later than 14 days after entry of judgment or the right is waived, and objections are due within 14 days after service of the memorandum. Attorney fees may be awarded when provided by statute or contract (with listed factors for amount), under Idaho Code section 12-121 only on a written finding of frivolous, unreasonable, or groundless conduct and never on a default judgment, and default judgments cannot exceed the amount or kind demanded in the pleadings.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"55","t":"Default; default judgment","s":"When a party against whom affirmative relief is sought fails to plead or defend, the court orders entry of default, and a party who has appeared must get 3 days’ written notice of the application. Default cannot be entered before the time to appear or defend expires, unless the party waives that time under oath and the court shortens it for good cause. A default judgment for a sum certain may be entered on the claimant’s request with an affidavit against a defendant who is not a minor or incompetent and was personally served (not by publication or outside Idaho); in other cases the party applies to the court, and an appearing party must get written notice at least 3 days before the hearing. The application must certify the defaulting party’s name and last-known address. Default may be set aside for good cause, a default judgment under Rule 60(b), and a default judgment against the State of Idaho or its officers, agencies, or subdivisions requires evidence satisfying the court.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"The court must grant summary judgment if the movant shows no genuine dispute of material fact and entitlement to judgment as a matter of law. A motion may be filed any time after 21 days from service of process on the adverse party (or that party’s appearance) or after the adverse party serves its own motion, but must be filed at least 90 days before the trial date or within 7 days of the order setting trial, whichever is later, unless the court orders otherwise. The motion, supporting documents, and brief must be served at least 28 days before the hearing, the answering brief and opposing documents at least 14 days before, and any reply brief at least 7 days before; the court may alter these times for good cause. Idaho’s deadlines differ from the federal rule’s, and the rule otherwise covers supporting facts with record citations, affidavits on personal knowledge, relief when facts are unavailable to the nonmovant, and sanctions for affidavits submitted in bad faith.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"The rules govern procedure for declaratory judgments under state statutes, and Rules 38 and 39 govern jury demands; another adequate remedy does not bar a declaratory judgment, and the court may order a speedy hearing. A party seeking a declaratory judgment on insurance policy coverage must give notice of the action to any person known to have a claim against the insured relating to the incident.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"Every judgment and amended judgment must be a separate document as required by Rule 54(a). Filing the judgment under Rule 5(d) or the clerk’s filing stamp constitutes entry, the judgment is not effective before entry, and entry must not be delayed for the taxing of costs.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"58.1","t":"Satisfaction of judgment","s":"On full payment of a judgment, the party in whose favor it was rendered must file a satisfaction of judgment in the court that entered it and record it in every county where the judgment or an abstract of it is recorded. The satisfaction must be signed by that party or the party’s attorney.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"58.2","t":"Certificate of adoption","s":"The court must not enter a decree of adoption until the adopting parents give the court a completed certificate of adoption on the form furnished by the department of vital statistics.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"59","t":"New trial; altering or amending a judgment","s":"The court may grant a new trial on all or some issues for listed grounds, including irregularity, jury misconduct, accident or surprise, newly discovered evidence, excessive or inadequate damages given under passion or prejudice, insufficient evidence, and legal error; motions on the first five grounds need a supporting affidavit and those on insufficiency or legal error must state factual grounds with particularity. A motion for new trial must be filed and served within 14 days after entry of judgment, supporting affidavits are filed with the motion, and the opponent has 14 days to serve opposing affidavits (extendable up to 21 more days by court order or written stipulation). Within 14 days after entry of judgment the court may on its own give notice of intent to order a new trial, and a motion to alter or amend a judgment must also be filed and served no later than 14 days after entry of judgment.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"59.1","t":"Additurs or remittiturs instead of new trial","s":"When a court conditionally grants or denies a new trial subject to an additur or remittitur, the party to whom it is directed has 42 days from entry of the order to accept or reject it, and filing a notice of appeal is neither acceptance nor rejection and defers that decision until the appeal is decided. If the conditional new trial order is affirmed on appeal, that party has 14 days from issuance of the appellate remittitur to accept or reject consistent with the appellate opinion.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from a judgment or order","s":"The court may correct clerical mistakes and oversights or omissions in a judgment, order, or record on motion or on its own, with or without notice, but once an appeal is docketed and pending only with the appellate court’s leave. On motion and just terms, the court may relieve a party from a final judgment for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect; newly discovered evidence that could not have been found in time to move for a new trial under Rule 59(b); fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct by an opposing party; a void judgment; a satisfied, released, or discharged judgment (or one based on a reversed or vacated judgment, or whose prospective application is no longer equitable); or any other reason justifying relief. The motion must be made within a reasonable time, and no more than 6 months after entry of the judgment or order for reasons (1), (2), and (3) (the federal rule allows a year), and it does not affect finality or suspend operation of the judgment. The rule does not limit the court’s power to entertain an independent action, to set aside within one year after entry a judgment against a party who was not personally served with summons and complaint and failed to appear, or to set aside a judgment for fraud on the court.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Unless justice requires otherwise, no error in admitting or excluding evidence or any other error by the court or a party is grounds for a new trial, setting aside a verdict, or vacating, modifying, or otherwise disturbing a judgment or order. At every stage the court must disregard errors and defects that do not affect any party’s substantial rights.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Unlike the federal rule, Idaho has no automatic stay on entry of judgment: execution and other enforcement may issue immediately unless the court, in its discretion and on conditions securing the adverse party, directs otherwise. Judgments in injunction, writ of mandate, and receivership actions are not stayed after entry or during appeal unless the court orders otherwise, and subdivision (c) governs suspending, modifying, restoring, or granting injunctions or writs of mandate during an appeal (on bond or other terms securing the opposing party’s rights). The court may stay enforcement, on terms securing the opposing party, pending disposition of a Rule 50, 52(b), 59, or 60 motion, and may stay enforcement of a Rule 54(b) judgment until a later judgment. Stays on appeal to the Supreme Court are governed by the Idaho Appellate Rules; no bond or security may be required from the state of Idaho or its officers, agencies, or subdivisions when it appeals; and parties may waive security by written stipulation. The rule does not limit the appellate court’s power to stay proceedings or preserve the status quo.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"63","t":"Judge’s inability to proceed","s":"If the trial judge cannot perform required duties after a verdict is returned or findings of fact and conclusions of law are filed, because of death, sickness, or other disability, any other judge regularly sitting in or assigned to the court where the action was tried may perform them. If that judge is satisfied the trial judge cannot perform the duties, the judge may grant a new trial.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizing a person or property","s":"At the start of and during an action, all remedies for seizing a person or property to secure satisfaction of a potential judgment are available under the circumstances and in the manner provided by law.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions and restraining orders","s":"A preliminary injunction may issue only on notice to the adverse party, and the court may advance and consolidate the trial on the merits with the hearing while preserving any jury right. A temporary restraining order may issue without notice only if an affidavit or verified complaint clearly shows immediate and irreparable injury before the adverse party can be heard and the movant or attorney certifies in writing efforts to give notice and why it should not be required. Such an order must state the date and hour issued and the reasons, be promptly filed, and expires at the time the court sets, not to exceed 14 days, unless extended for good cause for a like period or the adverse party consents to a longer extension; the preliminary-injunction hearing must then be set at the earliest possible time, and the adverse party may move to dissolve or modify on 2 days’ notice (or shorter notice the court sets). The movant must give security in an amount the court considers proper (not required of the State of Idaho or its political subdivisions, officers, and agencies); every injunction must state its reasons and terms and describe the restrained acts in reasonable detail, and binds only parties, their officers, agents, servants, employees, attorneys, and persons in active concert who have actual notice. Subdivision (e) lists Idaho-specific grounds for a preliminary injunction, and subdivision (f) lets district courts issue a writ restoring possession of real property taken by force, violence, fraud, or stealth, only on 7 days’ written notice of the hearing. Statutes on restraining orders in employer-employee labor disputes are not modified.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"66","t":"Sureties on bond","s":"A bond or undertaking required by statute or the rules must follow the form and surety justification requirements of chapter 6 of title 12, Idaho Code. An attorney of record, or a member or associate of a firm of record, cannot serve as surety. Each surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction and appoints the court clerk as agent for service, and liability may be enforced on motion without an independent action, with the clerk promptly mailing copies to every surety whose address is known.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit into court","s":"When relief sought includes a money judgment or disposition of money or a deliverable thing, a party may, on notice to every other party and with leave of court, deposit all or part of it with the court (whether or not the party claims it) and must deliver a copy of the order permitting the deposit to the clerk. On motion, the court may order a party holding money or a thing that is held as trustee for, or belongs or is due to, another party to deposit it in court or deliver it to the other party on just conditions. Deposits and withdrawals may be made only as the court directs or Idaho statutes provide.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"At least 14 days before trial, a party defending against a claim may serve (but not file) an offer to allow judgment on specified terms, deemed to include all costs and fees accrued; if the opposing party serves written acceptance within 14 days after service, either party may file the offer, acceptance, and proof of service, and the clerk enters judgment for the offer amount without costs. An unaccepted offer is withdrawn and inadmissible except in a proceeding to determine costs, and after liability is determined but not its extent, an offer must be served at least 14 days before the hearing on extent. Unlike the federal rule, Idaho compares the offer to an “adjusted award” (verdict plus the offeree’s pre-offer Rule 54(d)(1) costs and Rule 54(e)(1) attorney fees, pro-rated in contingent fee cases) for money damages claims: if the adjusted award is less than the offer, the offeree pays the offeror’s post-offer costs and the offeror pays the offeree’s pre-offer costs, but the offeror owes no post-offer costs or fees; if more, the offeror pays all of the offeree’s costs. For non-monetary claims, the comparison is offer versus judgment, with the offeree paying post-offer costs if the judgment is not more favorable and the offeror paying all costs if it is more favorable.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"An appealable final judgment (or a Rule 54(b) partial judgment) or court order for payment of money is enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise. A writ may not issue for more than the judgment’s face amount plus court-approved costs and fees without an affidavit of the party or attorney verifying the computation, and the clerk may rely on that affidavit. After service the sheriff must return the writ and report service fees, and uncollected fees are added to the judgment by the clerk. Procedure on execution and supplementary proceedings follows Idaho statutes and these rules, and the judgment creditor or successor in interest may use discovery from any person, including the judgment debtor, in aid of the judgment or execution.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"69.1","t":"Claim of exemptions; interrogatories submitted to garnishee; forms","s":"The forms for notice of exemptions, instructions to debtors and third parties, claim of exemptions or third-party claim, and interrogatories submitted to a garnishee must be in substantially the form found in Appendix B. This rule has no federal counterpart.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"69.2","t":"Deposition in aid of enforcing judgment","s":"After return of a writ of execution that does not fully satisfy a judgment debt, the judgment creditor may move to depose the judgment debtor and require production of documents and things bearing on ability to pay, and the presiding judge shall order the debtor to appear and produce what the judge considers appropriate. If the debtor resides in the county where the judgment was obtained, the deposition may be held at the county courthouse, at another location arranged by the creditor, or by video conference; if outside the county, at a location arranged by the creditor in the debtor’s county of residence or by video conference. In either case the presiding judge or a designated judge may appear, administer the oath, and officiate by video conference. This rule has no federal counterpart; adopted effective March 25, 2025.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"70","t":"Enforcing a judgment for a specific act","s":"If a party fails to comply with a judgment to convey land, deliver a deed or document, or perform another specific act within the time specified, the court may order another person appointed by the court to do it at the disobedient party’s expense, with the same effect as if the party had done it. If property is within the district, the court may instead enter a judgment divesting title and vesting it in others, with the effect of a legal conveyance. On application, the clerk must issue a writ of attachment or sequestration to compel obedience, or a writ of execution or assistance to enforce a judgment or order for possession, and noncompliance may also be the basis of a contempt motion.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"71","t":"Enforcing relief for or against a nonparty","s":"When an order grants relief for a nonparty or may be enforced against a nonparty, the procedure for enforcing it is the same as for a party.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"72","t":"Order to show cause","s":"An application for an order to show cause must be by verified complaint or accompanied by an affidavit stating the facts and grounds. If the application makes a prima facie showing for an order commanding specific acts, forbearance, or payment of money, the court must issue an order to show cause, which must be served on the party or the party’s attorney of record at least 7 days before the hearing in the same manner as a notice of hearing of a motion. Any party may elect to present testimony or cross-examine the adverse party and affiants by giving notice at least 24 hours before the hearing designating the persons to be cross-examined, and the order to show cause must be served with written notice of this requirement. If notice is timely given, the adverse party must have the designated persons present unless the court orders otherwise, or the court may impose sanctions including attorney fees. Contempt proceedings must be brought under Rule 75. This rule has no federal counterpart.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"73","t":"Receivers","s":"These rules govern an action in which appointment of a receiver is sought or a receiver sues or is sued. Appointment and administration of estates by receivers or similar officers must follow Idaho Code, and an action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"74","t":"Actions for writ of mandate or prohibition","s":"The Rules of Civil Procedure apply to actions for a writ of mandate or prohibition, and the rule defines writ of mandate, writ of prohibition, alternative writ, and peremptory writ. A court may issue an alternative writ on a verified complaint or affidavit showing grounds when the petition asks for one first; copies of the summons, petition, affidavits, and alternative writ must be served on the defendant at least 14 days before any show cause hearing. No contested trial of the peremptory writ petition may be had at the show cause hearing and no peremptory writ may issue from a contested show cause hearing; if the served party appears, the court must set the action for trial on the merits and may hear limited testimony on whether the alternative writ stays in force. For a peremptory writ, a responsive pleading is filed and served like any other answer, the court may order a jury trial on essential disputed facts, and if the writ is awarded on entry of judgment it must issue immediately as a peremptory writ. This rule is Idaho-specific.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"75","t":"Contempt","s":"This rule governs contempt proceedings in or apart from a civil lawsuit, but not contempt charged under Idaho Code section 18-1801 or other criminal statutes, and it defines summary and nonsummary proceedings and civil (conditional) and criminal (unconditional) sanctions. A summary proceeding may be used only for contempt in the court’s presence, after notice (which may be oral) and a brief chance to be heard, followed by a prompt signed written order reciting the facts. Nonsummary proceedings start by a judge’s written charge or by motion and affidavit (not an order to show cause) alleging each instance with specific facts, and notice of the time, date, and place to appear with the commencing documents must be served at least 7 days before the initial appearance unless the court orders otherwise (Rule 5(b) service on a party, Rule 4 on a nonparty). The rule sets the advice given at first appearance, warrant of attachment and bail requirements, the plea (a denial must be set for trial with at least 14 days to prepare), and a written response asserting affirmative defenses within 7 days after a denial plea. Civil sanctions require proof by a preponderance and present ability to comply, criminal sanctions require proof beyond a reasonable doubt and extra trial rights, consecutive criminal sanctions totaling more than 6 months of jail require a jury trial right, incarceration requires counsel or waiver, and the court may award costs and attorney fees under Idaho Code section 7-610 using Rule 54(e); amended effective July 1, 2018. This rule has no federal counterpart.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"76","t":"Small Lawsuit Resolution Act procedure","s":"This Idaho-specific rule applies only when a party initiates the Small Lawsuit Resolution Act (Idaho Code section 7-1501 et seq.), with the Rules of Civil Procedure otherwise applying. The Act’s dollar limit applies separately to each party and excludes costs and attorney fees, and the complaint must state the claim does not exceed the limit. A party starts the process by filing a “Notice of Initiation of Proceedings Under the Small Lawsuit Resolution Act” within the statutory time limits, accompanied by a completed case information sheet; an objection must be filed within 7 days of that notice or the opposing party is deemed to agree. Unless the parties agreed on an evaluator, the clerk gives each party a list of 5 randomly selected evaluators (10 if more than two parties), parties pay the private evaluator equally unless stipulated or ordered otherwise, and any party may request a trial de novo within 21 days after the notice of the evaluator’s decision is filed, with a case information sheet; each party must also file an information sheet when judgment is entered.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"77","t":"Class actions","s":"Class suits require numerosity, common questions, typicality, and adequate representation, and must also fit a (b)(1), (b)(2), or (b)(3) category (the latter requiring predominance and superiority). The court must decide certification at an early practicable time and may alter the order before final judgment; (b)(3) classes require the best practicable notice with individual notice to identifiable members, including the right to request exclusion. Settlement, voluntary dismissal, or compromise of a certified class requires court approval after notice and, if binding, a hearing and a finding that it is fair, reasonable, and adequate, and subdivision (f) is reserved (so Idaho has no counterpart to the federal rule’s certification appeal provision). The court must appoint class counsel, may award fees and nontaxable costs on a Rule 54 motion, and under Idaho’s subdivision (i), unless the parties jointly direct otherwise or statute controls, residual funds go to the Idaho Law Foundation for legal services for low-income Idahoans; amended effective January 1, 2026.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"78","t":"Derivative actions","s":"This rule applies when shareholders or members of a corporation or unincorporated association sue to enforce a right the entity failed to enforce, and the action cannot be maintained if the plaintiff does not fairly and adequately represent similarly situated shareholders or members. The complaint must be verified, allege that the plaintiff held the shares or membership when the transaction occurred (or received them by operation of law) and that the action is not collusive to confer jurisdiction, and state with particularity any effort to obtain action from directors or comparable authority and, if necessary, shareholders or members, or why no effort was made. The action may be settled, voluntarily dismissed, or compromised only with court approval, with notice to shareholders or members as the court orders.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"79","t":"Action relating to unincorporated associations","s":"This rule applies to actions brought by or against members of an unincorporated association as a class through named representative parties, which may be maintained only if those parties will fairly and adequately protect the interests of the association and its members. The court may issue orders corresponding to those under Rule 77(d), and settlement, voluntary dismissal, or compromise must follow the procedure in Rule 77(e). This rule is Idaho-specific.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"83","t":"Appeals from decisions of magistrate judges","s":"Appeals from magistrate court go to the Supreme Court as of right for final judgments on termination of parental rights or adoption petitions, by permission in specified custody, Child Protective Act, and guardianship matters, and when a magistrate is sitting as a district court; other final judgments and listed orders go to the district court. A notice of appeal must be filed with the district court clerk within 42 days of the file-stamped judgment or order (time restarts after a timely motion for JNOV, to amend findings, to alter or amend judgment, or for new trial), and a cross appeal may be filed within 14 days of service of the notice when the appeal was filed more than 28 days after entry; failure to meet these limits is jurisdictional and requires dismissal. The notice must be served immediately on the magistrate court and parties, with a preliminary statement of issues that may be filed within 14 days after the notice. Filing automatically stays proceedings and execution for 14 days (except civil protection orders), the appellant must pay the estimated transcript fee within 14 days of filing, the transcriber must lodge the transcript within 35 days of payment, parties have 21 days to object to a transcript or move to augment, the clerk must file the record within 7 days of settlement, motions need a supporting brief with a 14-day response time, separate transcript copies cost $1.00 per page, and if no further appeal is filed within 42 days after the appellate decision the clerk issues a remittitur; amended effective July 1, 2026.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"83.1","t":"Appeal by permission from interlocutory orders of magistrate judges","s":"Adopted effective July 1, 2024. Move in the magistrate court within 14 days after entry of the interlocutory order (controlling question of law, substantial ground for difference of opinion, immediate appeal may materially advance the case); the magistrate rules within 14 days after the hearing. Any party may then move the district court within 14 days after that ruling, or if the magistrate has not ruled within 21 days after the motion was filed; the appeal is valid only when the district court accepts it and sets a time for the notice of appeal. A district court denial is final, and no permission request goes to the Supreme Court except as Rule 83(a) and I.A.R. 12.1 allow.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"},{"n":"84","t":"Judicial review of agency actions by the district court","s":"This rule governs district court judicial review of state agency and local government actions when a statute authorizes review and supplies no procedure or standard of its own. The petition must be filed within 28 days after the agency action is ripe for review unless a statute says otherwise (time restarts after a decision on a timely reconsideration motion), cross-petitions are due within 14 days of service or within the original time, whichever is later, and the statement of issues may be filed within 14 days after the petition. Filing a petition is jurisdictional, and the petition must contain the agency name, record and transcript details, a transcript designation, and a certification of service and payment of estimated record and transcript fees. The agency clerk must lodge the record with the agency within 14 days of the petition, a transcriber must lodge a transcript within 14 days, parties have 14 days to object to the transcript and record (the agency must rule on objections within 14 days), and the agency must transmit the settled record to the district court within 42 days of service of the petition; transcripts or copies otherwise cost $1.00 per page where no fee is set. A motion to augment the record or to present additional evidence is due within 21 days of filing the settled record, motions need a supporting brief with a 14-day response time, filing does not automatically stay agency action, and the district court’s judgment becomes final 42 days after the file-stamped judgment (or order denying rehearing or deciding a post-judgment motion) if no appeal is filed, after which the clerk issues a remittitur.","u":"https://isc.idaho.gov/rules-procedure/ircp"}]});
